{"users":[],"notes":[{"id":"mat-mathematics-01","title":"Number, Ratio and Proportion: Number skills for problem solving","subject":"Mathematics","topic":"Number, Ratio and Proportion","body":"<h2>Core ideas</h2><p>Use fractions, decimals, percentages, ratios and proportions to represent quantities in different ways. Convert carefully between forms and keep units consistent.</p><h3>Key methods</h3><ul><li>Percentage change = change ÷ original × 100.</li><li>For a ratio a:b, multiply both parts by the same scale factor.</li><li>For direct proportion, y = kx; find k before solving for a new value.</li></ul><h3>Worked example</h3><p>If 3 notebooks cost ₦1,500, one notebook costs ₦500. Therefore 8 notebooks cost ₦4,000, assuming a constant unit price.</p><p><b>Practice tip:</b> write the units beside every quantity before calculating.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Number skills for problem solving</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Keep units visible and use estimation or substitution to verify numerical answers.</li><li>Check all possible algebraic solutions against the original equation.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-mathematics-02","title":"Algebraic Expressions and Equations: Building and solving equations","subject":"Mathematics","topic":"Algebraic Expressions and Equations","body":"<h2>Core ideas</h2><p>Algebra uses symbols to represent unknown or variable quantities. Simplify by collecting like terms, then solve equations using inverse operations.</p><h3>Key rules</h3><ul><li>3x + 2x = 5x, but 3x + 2 cannot be combined.</li><li>Expand brackets by multiplying every term inside by the outside factor.</li><li>Keep both sides of an equation balanced when adding, subtracting, multiplying or dividing.</li></ul><p><b>Example:</b> 2(x + 4) = 18 → x + 4 = 9 → x = 5.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Building and solving equations</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Keep units visible and use estimation or substitution to verify numerical answers.</li><li>Check all possible algebraic solutions against the original equation.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-mathematics-03","title":"Geometry and Mensuration: Angles, shapes, perimeter, area and volume","subject":"Mathematics","topic":"Geometry and Mensuration","body":"<h2>Core ideas</h2><p>Angles, perimeter, area and volume describe geometric relationships and measurements.</p><ul><li>Triangle angles sum to 180°.</li><li>Quadrilateral interior angles sum to 360°.</li><li>Rectangle area = length × width.</li><li>Circle area = πr² and circumference = 2πr.</li><li>For a right triangle, a² + b² = c².</li></ul><p>Always state square units for area and cubic units for volume.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Angles, shapes, perimeter, area and volume</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Keep units visible and use estimation or substitution to verify numerical answers.</li><li>Check all possible algebraic solutions against the original equation.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-mathematics-04","title":"Statistics and Probability: Organising data and calculating chance","subject":"Mathematics","topic":"Statistics and Probability","body":"<h2>Core ideas</h2><p>Statistics summarises data. The mean is total ÷ number of observations; the median is the middle value after ordering; the mode is the most frequent value.</p><p>Probability measures chance from 0 to 1. For equally likely outcomes, probability = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes.</p><p>When solving data questions, first identify whether the question asks for a measure of centre, spread, or chance.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Organising data and calculating chance</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Keep units visible and use estimation or substitution to verify numerical answers.</li><li>Check all possible algebraic solutions against the original equation.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-mathematics-05","title":"Graphs, Sequences and Functions: Patterns, coordinates and relationships","subject":"Mathematics","topic":"Graphs, Sequences and Functions","body":"<h2>Core ideas</h2><p>Sequences follow rules. An arithmetic sequence changes by a constant difference. On a coordinate plane, the first number is x and the second is y.</p><p>For a straight line y = mx + c, m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept. A positive gradient rises from left to right; a negative gradient falls.</p><p>Use a table of values to check a suspected function rule before drawing a graph.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Patterns, coordinates and relationships</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Keep units visible and use estimation or substitution to verify numerical answers.</li><li>Check all possible algebraic solutions against the original equation.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-use-of-english-01","title":"Vocabulary and Word Choice: Synonyms, antonyms, context and precise usage","subject":"Use of English","topic":"Vocabulary and Word Choice","body":"<h2>Vocabulary</h2><p>Strong communication depends on choosing words that match meaning, register and context. Distinguish synonyms that are close in meaning from words that are interchangeable in every context.</p><h3>Study routine</h3><ul><li>Record new words with a definition and sentence.</li><li>Learn common prefixes, suffixes and roots.</li><li>Use context clues before consulting a dictionary.</li></ul><p>In examinations, eliminate options that do not fit the grammatical or semantic context.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Synonyms, antonyms, context and precise usage</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Identify the grammatical subject before checking concord.</li><li>In comprehension, separate explicit evidence from reasonable inference.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-use-of-english-02","title":"Grammar and Concord: Agreement, tense, clauses and sentence structure","subject":"Use of English","topic":"Grammar and Concord","body":"<h2>Grammar and concord</h2><p>Subject–verb agreement requires the verb to match the grammatical subject, not a distracting noun nearby. Pay attention to collective nouns, neither/nor structures and expressions such as “a number of”.</p><p>Review tense, pronouns, articles, prepositions and clause structure. A sentence should be complete and logically connected.</p><p><b>Example:</b> “The list of candidates is ready.” The subject is “list”, not “candidates”.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Agreement, tense, clauses and sentence structure</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Identify the grammatical subject before checking concord.</li><li>In comprehension, separate explicit evidence from reasonable inference.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-use-of-english-03","title":"Comprehension Skills: Main ideas, inference, tone and evidence","subject":"Use of English","topic":"Comprehension Skills","body":"<h2>Comprehension</h2><p>Read first for the overall argument, then return to the passage for evidence. Separate what the writer states directly from what can reasonably be inferred.</p><ul><li>Main idea: what the passage is chiefly about.</li><li>Inference: a conclusion supported by clues in the text.</li><li>Tone: the writer’s attitude.</li><li>Purpose: why the writer produced the text.</li></ul><p>For evidence questions, choose the option most directly supported by the passage.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Main ideas, inference, tone and evidence</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Identify the grammatical subject before checking concord.</li><li>In comprehension, separate explicit evidence from reasonable inference.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-use-of-english-04","title":"Figures of Speech and Idioms: Meaning and effect of figurative language","subject":"Use of English","topic":"Figures of Speech and Idioms","body":"<h2>Figurative language</h2><p>A simile compares using “like” or “as”; a metaphor makes a direct comparison; personification gives human qualities to non-human things; hyperbole uses deliberate exaggeration.</p><p>Idioms should be interpreted by their conventional meaning, not literally. For example, “break the ice” means to make an interaction more comfortable.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Meaning and effect of figurative language</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Identify the grammatical subject before checking concord.</li><li>In comprehension, separate explicit evidence from reasonable inference.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-use-of-english-05","title":"Writing and Communication: Paragraphs, formal writing, punctuation and coherence","subject":"Use of English","topic":"Writing and Communication","body":"<h2>Effective writing</h2><p>A strong paragraph has a clear controlling idea, supporting details, logical progression and an appropriate concluding link. Use punctuation to make relationships between ideas clear.</p><p>Formal communication should be respectful, precise and appropriately structured. Avoid unnecessary slang and unexplained abbreviations.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Paragraphs, formal writing, punctuation and coherence</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Identify the grammatical subject before checking concord.</li><li>In comprehension, separate explicit evidence from reasonable inference.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-physics-01","title":"Mechanics: Motion, forces, work, energy and momentum","subject":"Physics","topic":"Mechanics","body":"<h2>Mechanics</h2><p>Motion is described using distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration. Force changes motion and is measured in newtons.</p><ul><li>Speed = distance ÷ time.</li><li>Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time.</li><li>F = ma.</li><li>Work = force × displacement in the force direction.</li><li>Kinetic energy = ½mv².</li><li>Momentum = mv.</li></ul><p>Always convert quantities to consistent SI units before calculating.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Motion, forces, work, energy and momentum</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Convert quantities to consistent SI units before substitution.</li><li>State assumptions such as negligible air resistance when the model requires them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-physics-02","title":"Waves and Optics: Wave properties, sound, light and lenses","subject":"Physics","topic":"Waves and Optics","body":"<h2>Waves and optics</h2><p>Wave frequency is the number of cycles per second and period is its reciprocal. Light is electromagnetic and can travel through a vacuum.</p><p>Reflection involves bouncing from a surface; refraction involves a change in speed and direction when light enters another medium. Convex lenses converge rays while concave lenses diverge them.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Wave properties, sound, light and lenses</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Convert quantities to consistent SI units before substitution.</li><li>State assumptions such as negligible air resistance when the model requires them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-physics-03","title":"Electricity: Current, voltage, resistance, power and safety","subject":"Physics","topic":"Electricity","body":"<h2>Electricity</h2><p>Current is the rate of flow of charge, voltage is energy transferred per unit charge, and resistance opposes current.</p><ul><li>V = IR.</li><li>P = VI.</li><li>E = Pt.</li></ul><p>Fuses protect circuits by melting when current becomes excessive. Ammeter connections are in series; voltmeters are connected across a component.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Current, voltage, resistance, power and safety</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Convert quantities to consistent SI units before substitution.</li><li>State assumptions such as negligible air resistance when the model requires them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-physics-04","title":"Thermal Physics: Temperature, heat transfer and gas behaviour","subject":"Physics","topic":"Thermal Physics","body":"<h2>Thermal physics</h2><p>Temperature measures the thermal state of a body, while heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Heat can be transferred by conduction, convection and radiation.</p><p>For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, pressure increases as volume decreases (Boyle’s law).</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Temperature, heat transfer and gas behaviour</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Convert quantities to consistent SI units before substitution.</li><li>State assumptions such as negligible air resistance when the model requires them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-physics-05","title":"Measurement and Practical Reasoning: Units, instruments, uncertainty and experimental thinking","subject":"Physics","topic":"Measurement and Practical Reasoning","body":"<h2>Measurement</h2><p>Good physics experiments use appropriate instruments, repeated measurements and controlled variables. Record units and sensible precision.</p><p>Distinguish accuracy from precision: accuracy concerns closeness to the accepted value, while precision concerns repeatability.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Units, instruments, uncertainty and experimental thinking</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Convert quantities to consistent SI units before substitution.</li><li>State assumptions such as negligible air resistance when the model requires them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-chemistry-01","title":"Atomic Structure and Periodicity: Particles, atomic number, isotopes and periodic trends","subject":"Chemistry","topic":"Atomic Structure and Periodicity","body":"<h2>Atomic structure</h2><p>Atoms contain protons and neutrons in the nucleus and electrons in shells around it. Atomic number equals proton number; mass number equals protons plus neutrons.</p><p>Elements in the same group often have related chemical properties because they have similar numbers of outer-shell electrons.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Particles, atomic number, isotopes and periodic trends</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Balance equations before using mole ratios.</li><li>Distinguish observations in a practical experiment from the conclusions drawn from them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-chemistry-02","title":"Chemical Bonding and Matter: Ionic, covalent and metallic bonding","subject":"Chemistry","topic":"Chemical Bonding and Matter","body":"<h2>Bonding</h2><p>Ionic bonding results from electron transfer, usually between metals and non-metals. Covalent bonding involves sharing electron pairs between atoms. Metallic bonding involves positive metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons.</p><p>Bonding helps explain properties such as melting point, conductivity and solubility.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Ionic, covalent and metallic bonding</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Balance equations before using mole ratios.</li><li>Distinguish observations in a practical experiment from the conclusions drawn from them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-chemistry-03","title":"Acids, Bases and Salts: pH, indicators, neutralisation and salts","subject":"Chemistry","topic":"Acids, Bases and Salts","body":"<h2>Acids and bases</h2><p>Acids have pH below 7; bases have pH above 7. Indicators provide colour changes that help estimate acidity or alkalinity.</p><p>Neutralisation commonly produces salt and water. Acid + carbonate also produces carbon dioxide.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>pH, indicators, neutralisation and salts</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Balance equations before using mole ratios.</li><li>Distinguish observations in a practical experiment from the conclusions drawn from them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-chemistry-04","title":"Organic Chemistry: Hydrocarbons, functional groups and separation","subject":"Chemistry","topic":"Organic Chemistry","body":"<h2>Organic chemistry</h2><p>Hydrocarbons contain carbon and hydrogen. Alkanes are saturated and have formula CnH2n+2; alkenes contain a carbon–carbon double bond and are unsaturated.</p><p>Crude oil is separated into fractions by fractional distillation because its components have different boiling ranges.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Hydrocarbons, functional groups and separation</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Balance equations before using mole ratios.</li><li>Distinguish observations in a practical experiment from the conclusions drawn from them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-chemistry-05","title":"Chemical Reactions and Quantitative Chemistry: Rates, energy, electrolysis and calculations","subject":"Chemistry","topic":"Chemical Reactions and Quantitative Chemistry","body":"<h2>Reactions</h2><p>Chemical reactions rearrange atoms. Catalysts speed reactions by providing a lower-activation-energy pathway and are not permanently consumed.</p><p>Exothermic reactions release heat; endothermic reactions absorb heat. Electrolysis uses electrical energy to drive chemical decomposition.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Rates, energy, electrolysis and calculations</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Balance equations before using mole ratios.</li><li>Distinguish observations in a practical experiment from the conclusions drawn from them.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-biology-01","title":"Cell Biology: Cell structures, transport and organisation","subject":"Biology","topic":"Cell Biology","body":"<h2>Cells</h2><p>Cells are the basic units of life. Eukaryotic cells contain a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. Plant cells also have a cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts and a large permanent vacuole.</p><p>Diffusion is net movement from high to low concentration. Osmosis is movement of water through a partially permeable membrane.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Cell structures, transport and organisation</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Explain how biological structure supports function.</li><li>Connect processes across levels of organisation, from cells to organisms and ecosystems.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-biology-02","title":"Nutrition and Digestion: Food classes, enzymes and absorption","subject":"Biology","topic":"Nutrition and Digestion","body":"<h2>Nutrition</h2><p>Carbohydrates provide energy, proteins support growth and repair, fats provide concentrated energy, while vitamins, minerals, fibre and water support normal body functions.</p><p>Digestive enzymes break large molecules into smaller soluble molecules. Most nutrient absorption occurs in the small intestine through villi.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Food classes, enzymes and absorption</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Explain how biological structure supports function.</li><li>Connect processes across levels of organisation, from cells to organisms and ecosystems.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-biology-03","title":"Transport and Respiration: Blood, circulation, gas exchange and energy release","subject":"Biology","topic":"Transport and Respiration","body":"<h2>Transport and respiration</h2><p>The heart pumps blood through the circulatory system. Red blood cells transport oxygen using haemoglobin; white blood cells support defence.</p><p>Cellular respiration releases usable energy from glucose. Aerobic respiration uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Blood, circulation, gas exchange and energy release</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Explain how biological structure supports function.</li><li>Connect processes across levels of organisation, from cells to organisms and ecosystems.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-biology-04","title":"Reproduction and Genetics: Human reproduction, inheritance and variation","subject":"Biology","topic":"Reproduction and Genetics","body":"<h2>Reproduction and genetics</h2><p>Sexual reproduction involves gametes and produces genetic variation. Fertilisation forms a zygote. Genes are sections of DNA; alleles are alternative forms of a gene.</p><p>Inheritance patterns can be represented using genetic diagrams or Punnett squares.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Human reproduction, inheritance and variation</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Explain how biological structure supports function.</li><li>Connect processes across levels of organisation, from cells to organisms and ecosystems.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"mat-biology-05","title":"Ecology and Health: Ecosystems, immunity, disease and conservation","subject":"Biology","topic":"Ecology and Health","body":"<h2>Ecology and health</h2><p>An ecosystem contains organisms and their physical environment. Producers capture energy, consumers feed on other organisms, and decomposers recycle nutrients.</p><p>Vaccination trains the immune system to respond rapidly to a pathogen. Public health also depends on sanitation, nutrition, disease surveillance and appropriate treatment.</p>\n<h2>Detailed Study Extension</h2>\n<p>This section expands <b>Ecosystems, immunity, disease and conservation</b> beyond the basic summary. The goal is to move from simple recall to explanation, application and examination-level reasoning.</p>\n<h3>Learning targets</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the central ideas in your own words rather than memorising isolated definitions.</li>\n<li>Apply the ideas to unfamiliar examples and real-life situations.</li>\n<li>Compare closely related concepts and justify which one applies to a given problem.</li>\n<li>Use evidence, calculations or grammatical reasoning to support an answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Core points to master</h3>\n<ul><li>Start by identifying the key terms, quantities or conditions before choosing a method.</li><li>Work through the idea step by step and record intermediate reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Compare similar concepts and state the distinction that determines which one applies.</li><li>Use an example or counterexample to test whether your understanding is correct.</li><li>Watch for limiting words such as always, only, most likely, best and except.</li><li>After each exercise, explain why the correct answer is correct and why the strongest distractor is wrong.</li><li>Apply the concept to an unfamiliar situation instead of relying only on memorised examples.</li><li>Keep a short error log and revisit recurring mistakes before the next practice session.</li><li>Explain how biological structure supports function.</li><li>Connect processes across levels of organisation, from cells to organisms and ecosystems.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to study this topic</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Preview:</b> write down what you already know and identify two concepts that are unclear.</li>\n<li><b>Work through:</b> study the explanations, examples and relationships in the note.</li>\n<li><b>Apply:</b> attempt at least five unfamiliar questions without looking at the answer.</li>\n<li><b>Explain:</b> teach one difficult idea aloud in simple language.</li>\n<li><b>Check:</b> revisit every error and record why the wrong option looked attractive.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Common examination traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing a familiar-looking answer without checking the exact wording.</li>\n<li>Ignoring units, conditions, exceptions or limiting words such as <i>always</i>, <i>only</i>, <i>most likely</i> and <i>best</i>.</li>\n<li>Confusing definition with application: knowing a term is not the same as recognising it in a new situation.</li>\n<li>Failing to review an answer after completing a multi-step problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Self-check questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What are the three most important ideas I should remember from this topic?</li>\n<li>Can I explain one difficult point without copying the note?</li>\n<li>What evidence, calculation or rule would I use to justify my answer?</li>\n<li>What is the most likely misconception a learner could have about this topic?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Exam preparation tip:</b> Use the Question Bank in <b>Study Mode</b> first to diagnose weak areas, then switch to <b>Practice Mode</b> to test speed, accuracy and endurance under time pressure.</p>\n","format":"html","bodyFormat":"html","attachments":[],"fileUrl":"","fileKey":"","fileName":"","published":true,"date":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z"}],"tests":[{"id":"test-mathematics-1","title":"Mathematics UTME Standard CBT 1","subject":"Mathematics","duration":50,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-mathematics-01","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A trader buys an article for ₦8,000 and spends ₦6,000 on transport. He then sells it for ₦15,000. What is the percentage profit on the total cost?","options":["7.14%","87.50%","6.67%","0.01%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total cost = purchase price + transport. Percentage profit = profit ÷ total cost × 100.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-02","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If x² -7x +12 = 0, what is the sum of the squares of its roots?","options":["25","49","12","27"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The roots are r₁ and r₂. Their squared sum is r₁²+r₂²=(r₁+r₂)²−2r₁r₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-03","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An arithmetic progression has first term 4 and common difference 3. What is its 9th term?","options":["28","31","25","63"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use Tₙ = a + (n−1)d.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-04","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Simplify 3^4 × 3^3.","options":["2187","531441","3","16384"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For equal bases, indices are added when multiplying powers.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-05","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sector has radius 5 cm and central angle 75°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":["125/24π cm²","25π cm²","125/12π cm²","120π cm²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sector area = θ/360 × πr².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-06","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Solve simultaneously: 3x + 2y = 17 and 2x + 3y = 18. What is the value of x?","options":["3","4","2","5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use elimination or substitution; the simultaneous equations give x as the stated value.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-07","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A box contains 4 red and 5 blue counters. Two counters are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","options":["1/6","4/9","5/18","8/9"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiply the probability of drawing a red first by the probability of drawing a red second without replacement.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-08","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Four test scores have mean 51. Three scores are 45, 55 and 48. What is the fourth score?","options":["56","51","59","53"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The total of four scores is 4×mean; subtract the three known scores.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-09","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A circular tank has radius 4 cm. What is the ratio of its circumference to its area?","options":["2/4","1/4","4/2","2/5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C/A = 2πr ÷ πr² = 2/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-10","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Find the square of the distance between (2, 3) and (6, 8).","options":["41","9","20","43"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use d²=(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-11","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A trader buys an article for ₦9,000 and spends ₦7,000 on transport. He then sells it for ₦17,000. What is the percentage profit on the total cost?","options":["6.25%","88.89%","5.88%","0.01%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total cost = purchase price + transport. Percentage profit = profit ÷ total cost × 100.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-12","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If x² -9x +20 = 0, what is the sum of the squares of its roots?","options":["41","81","20","43"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The roots are r₁ and r₂. Their squared sum is r₁²+r₂²=(r₁+r₂)²−2r₁r₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-13","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An arithmetic progression has first term 5 and common difference 4. What is its 10th term?","options":["41","45","37","90"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use Tₙ = a + (n−1)d.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-14","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Simplify 4^5 × 4^4.","options":["262144","1099511627776","4","1953125"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For equal bases, indices are added when multiplying powers.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-15","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sector has radius 6 cm and central angle 90°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":["9π cm²","36π cm²","18π cm²","144π cm²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sector area = θ/360 × πr².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-16","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Solve simultaneously: 4x + 3y = 31 and 3x + 4y = 32. What is the value of x?","options":["4","5","3","6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use elimination or substitution; the simultaneous equations give x as the stated value.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-17","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A box contains 5 red and 6 blue counters. Two counters are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","options":["2/11","5/11","3/11","10/11"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiply the probability of drawi