Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An arithmetic progression has first term 22 and common difference 7. What is its 27th term?","options":["204","211","197","783"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use Tₙ = a + (n−1)d.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Simplify 21^7 × 21^5.","options":["7355827511386641","18952884486433699020098042171468383867092598701","441","12855002631049216"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For equal bases, indices are added when multiplying powers.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sector has radius 23 cm and central angle 120°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":["529/3π cm²","529π cm²","1058/3π cm²","1587π cm²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sector area = θ/360 × πr².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Solve simultaneously: 6x + 2y = 170 and 2x + 6y = 174. What is the value of x?","options":["21","22","20","23"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use elimination or substitution; the simultaneous equations give x as the stated value.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A box contains 22 red and 23 blue counters. Two counters are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","options":["7/30","22/45","23/90","44/45"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiply the probability of drawing a red first by the probability of drawing a red second without replacement.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Four test scores have mean 69. Three scores are 63, 73 and 66. What is the fourth score?","options":["74","69","77","71"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The total of four scores is 4×mean; subtract the three known scores.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A circular tank has radius 22 cm. What is the ratio of its circumference to its area?","options":["2/22","1/22","22/2","2/23"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C/A = 2πr ÷ πr² = 2/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Find the square of the distance between (6, 5) and (12, 13).","options":["100","14","48","102"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use d²=(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1015","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A trader buys an article for ₦27,000 and spends ₦25,000 on transport. He then sells it for ₦53,000. What is the percentage profit on the total cost?","options":["1.92%","96.30%","1.89%","0.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total cost = purchase price + transport. Percentage profit = profit ÷ total cost × 100.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If x² -45x +506 = 0, what is the sum of the squares of its roots?","options":["1013","2025","506","1015"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The roots are r₁ and r₂. Their squared sum is r₁²+r₂²=(r₁+r₂)²−2r₁r₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An arithmetic progression has first term 23 and common difference 8. What is its 28th term?","options":["239","247","231","868"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use Tₙ = a + (n−1)d.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Simplify 22^3 × 22^2.","options":["5153632","113379904","22","6436343"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For equal bases, indices are added when multiplying powers.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1019","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sector has radius 24 cm and central angle 60°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":["96π cm²","576π cm²","192π cm²","3456π cm²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sector area = θ/360 × πr².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1020","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Solve simultaneously: 2x + 3y = 113 and 3x + 2y = 112. What is the value of x?","options":["22","23","21","24"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use elimination or substitution; the simultaneous equations give x as the stated value.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A box contains 23 red and 24 blue counters. Two counters are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","options":["11/47","23/47","12/47","46/47"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Multiply the probability of drawing a red first by the probability of drawing a red second without replacement.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Four test scores have mean 70. Three scores are 64, 74 and 67. What is the fourth score?","options":["75","70","78","72"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The total of four scores is 4×mean; subtract the three known scores.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A circular tank has radius 23 cm. What is the ratio of its circumference to its area?","options":["2/23","1/23","23/2","2/24"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C/A = 2πr ÷ πr² = 2/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-mathematics-gen-1024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Find the square of the distance between (7, 6) and (10, 10).","options":["25","7","12","27"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use d²=(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Mathematics","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-use-of-english-1","title":"Use of English UTME Standard CBT 1","subject":"Use of English","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-001","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P01","passage":"A community library is more than a room filled with books. When it is thoughtfully managed, it gives children access to stories, reference works and digital resources that may not be available at home. It also provides a quiet place for study. Yet a library does not become useful merely because shelves are full. Readers need guidance, relevant materials and programmes that encourage regular use. In one town, volunteers organised reading circles and invited local writers to speak. Attendance increased because the library became a place where people could discuss ideas, not simply borrow books. The experience suggests that access to information is strongest when it is combined with opportunities to use and share knowledge.","q":"The passage is mainly concerned with:","options":["fullness of shelves alone","a historical account of the topic","a rejection of the topic altogether","a list of unrelated examples"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The passage focuses on fullness of shelves alone.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-002","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P02","passage":"Rainwater harvesting can reduce pressure on public water supplies, especially during periods when rainfall is seasonal. A simple system collects water from a roof, directs it through a filter and stores it in a covered container. The stored water may be used for gardening, cleaning or other purposes when it is safe to do so. However, harvesting is not a complete answer to every water problem. Poorly maintained containers can become contaminated, while excessive dependence on stored rainwater may create shortages during unusually long dry periods. The most sensible approach is therefore to treat harvested water as one component of a broader water-management strategy.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'excessive' is closest in meaning to:","options":["excessive","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'excessive' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-003","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P07","passage":"Peer tutoring is sometimes misunderstood as a way of replacing the teacher. In reality, a well-designed peer-tutoring activity gives learners structured opportunities to explain ideas to one another while the teacher monitors the process. The tutor also benefits because explaining a concept can expose gaps in his or her own understanding. However, simply pairing a stronger student with a weaker one is not enough. Both students need clear roles, appropriate tasks and feedback. Peer tutoring works best when interaction is purposeful rather than improvised.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'improvised' is closest in meaning to:","options":["improvised","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'improvised' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-004","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P12","passage":"A timetable is often regarded as a fixed grid, but its quality depends on what happens within the periods it creates. Two schools may allocate the same number of hours to a subject while producing different learning experiences because one protects preparation time and balances demanding activities with less intensive ones. A timetable should therefore be seen as a planning tool rather than merely a list of periods. Its effectiveness lies in how well it supports the rhythm of teaching and learning.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'intensive' is closest in meaning to:","options":["intensive","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'intensive' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-005","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P17","passage":"A product labelled recyclable is not necessarily recycled in practice. Recycling depends on collection systems, sorting facilities, market demand for recovered materials and consumer behaviour. A material may be technically recyclable yet rarely accepted by local collectors. This distinction matters because labels can create the impression that disposal has no environmental consequences. Responsible communication should therefore distinguish between what is technically possible and what is routinely achieved.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'routinely' is closest in meaning to:","options":["routinely","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'routinely' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Neither the principal nor the teachers ____ willing to postpone the examination.","options":["was","is","were","has been"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'teachers'; therefore 'were'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the word that is nearest in meaning to 'parsimonious'.","options":["frugal","reckless","eloquent","generous"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parsimonious means excessively unwilling to spend or give; 'frugal' is the closest option.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> I. If I were you, I would apologise.<br><b>II.</b> II. If I was you, I would apologise.<br><b>III.</b> III. He has lived here since 2020.<br><b>IV.</b> IV. He is living here since 2020.","options":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The irrealis form is 'were', and 'since 2020' calls for present perfect in this meaning.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option contains a correctly formed parallel structure?","options":["The programme aims to improve reading, numeracy and children think critically.","The programme aims to improve reading, numeracy and critical thinking.","The programme aims improving reading, numeracy and critical thinking.","The programme aims to improve reading, numeracy and to think critically."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The coordinated items are all noun phrases: reading, numeracy and critical thinking.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the best interpretation of: 'The minister's statement was deliberately equivocal.'","options":["It was deliberately clear.","It was deliberately ambiguous.","It was accidentally humorous.","It was legally binding."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equivocal means open to more than one interpretation or deliberately ambiguous.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option best explains why a comma is needed in 'My brother, who lives in Ibadan, is a doctor.'?","options":["The clause is restrictive.","The clause is non-restrictive and adds extra information.","The comma separates subject and verb.","The clause is an adverbial of time."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The relative clause provides additional, non-essential information and is therefore set off by commas.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Neither the principal nor the teachers ____ willing to postpone the examination.","options":["was","were","has been","is"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'teachers'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The opposite of 'meticulous' is:","options":["careless","precise","thorough","exact"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Meticulous means very careful and precise.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose a word nearest in meaning to 'parsimonious'.","options":["frugal","reckless","talkative","generous"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parsimonious means excessively unwilling to spend.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-015","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to \"hostile\" is","options":["friendly","violent","severe","bitter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “friendly” because it is the closest opposite in meaning to “hostile”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","options":["After the lecture, the students discussed the experiment.","After the lecture the students, discussed the experiment.","After, the lecture the students discussed the experiment.","After the lecture the students discussed, the experiment."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct option is “After the lecture, the students discussed the experiment.” because it places the punctuation marks at the correct grammatical boundaries.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the word nearest in meaning to \"parsimonious\"","options":["wasteful","economical","generous","careless"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “economical” because it is the closest synonym of “parsimonious” in the intended context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: No sooner had the bell rung ____ the candidates entered","options":["than","when","that","while"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “than” because the correlative construction is “no sooner … than”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-019","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The witness was accused ____ withholding relevant evidence","options":["of","for","with","on"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “of” because the standard construction is “accused of” followed by the alleged offence or action.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-020","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P01","passage":"A community library is more than a room filled with books. When it is thoughtfully managed, it gives children access to stories, reference works and digital resources that may not be available at home. It also provides a quiet place for study. Yet a library does not become useful merely because shelves are full. Readers need guidance, relevant materials and programmes that encourage regular use. In one town, volunteers organised reading circles and invited local writers to speak. Attendance increased because the library became a place where people could discuss ideas, not simply borrow books. The experience suggests that access to information is strongest when it is combined with opportunities to use and share knowledge.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'thoughtfully' is closest in meaning to:","options":["thoughtfully","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'thoughtfully' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-021","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P03","passage":"A school garden can turn an abstract lesson into a visible experience. Instead of merely reading about germination, pupils can plant seeds, observe changes and record measurements. The value of the garden, however, does not depend on producing a large harvest. Its educational importance lies in the questions it generates: Why did one seedling grow faster? What happens when water is withheld? How does soil affect roots? When teachers use these questions to guide investigation, the garden becomes a learning environment rather than an ornamental project. Its success should therefore be judged partly by the quality of children's observations and explanations.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'ornamental' is closest in meaning to:","options":["ornamental","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'ornamental' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-022","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P08","passage":"A local market may appear inefficient because many sellers offer similar goods, yet duplication can serve a useful purpose. Competition can give buyers alternatives and encourage sellers to improve quality or adjust prices. The market also carries information that formal statistics may miss: traders notice changes in demand quickly and adapt their stock accordingly. This does not mean markets always operate fairly. Access to storage, transport and credit can give some sellers advantages over others. Understanding a market therefore requires attention to both competition and the conditions under which competition occurs.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'duplication' is closest in meaning to:","options":["duplication","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'duplication' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-023","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P13","passage":"Water conservation is frequently discussed as though individual behaviour alone determines consumption. Household habits matter, but infrastructure can be equally important. A leaking distribution pipe may waste far more water than a careful household can save by turning off a tap quickly. This does not make personal responsibility irrelevant. Rather, it shows that conservation requires action at several levels, from household practice to system maintenance.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'irrelevant' is closest in meaning to:","options":["irrelevant","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'irrelevant' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-024","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P18","passage":"Weather forecasts are predictions based on observations and models, not promises about exactly what will happen in every location. Their usefulness depends partly on the time range considered: forecasts generally become less certain as the horizon extends. A forecast of heavy rain tomorrow may therefore justify preparation even though the precise timing and amount remain uncertain. Treating uncertainty as a reason to ignore forecasts misunderstands how prediction works.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'horizon' is closest in meaning to:","options":["horizon","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'horizon' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word 'intransigent' is closest in meaning to:","options":["easily persuaded","uncompromising","carelessly generous","deeply confused"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Intransigent describes someone unwilling to change a position or compromise.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option in which the phrasal verb is used correctly.","options":["The new policy will come into effect next month.","The new policy will come into affect next month.","The new policy will came into effect next month.","The new policy will come effect into next month."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct collocation is 'come into effect', meaning become operational.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> I. The principal congratulated Ada and me.<br><b>II.</b> II. The principal congratulated Ada and I.<br><b>III.</b> III. Between you and me, the plan is risky.<br><b>IV.</b> IV. Between you and I, the plan is risky.","options":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"After a verb or preposition, the objective pronoun is required: 'me'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best expresses the meaning: 'The committee's decision was not without controversy.'","options":["The decision was universally accepted.","The decision generated some controversy.","The decision was completely secret.","The committee made no decision."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The construction 'not without controversy' indicates that controversy did occur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-029","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence contains a dangling modifier?","options":["Walking to school, the rain soaked Tunde.","Walking to school, Tunde was soaked by the rain.","Tunde was soaked by the rain while walking to school.","Because Tunde walked to school, the rain soaked his clothes."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The opening phrase logically modifies Tunde, not the rain; therefore the first sentence dangles.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-030","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the best word: The evidence was so ____ that the panel could not reasonably ignore it.","options":["compelling","compliant","complacent","compensatory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Compelling evidence is persuasive and difficult to disregard.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If the committee ____ earlier, the dispute might have been avoided.","options":["acts","acted","had acted","has acted"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The third conditional uses the past perfect in the if-clause.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-032","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","options":["However, the proposal was rejected.","Nevertheless, the proposal was rejected.","However; the proposal was rejected.","The proposal was rejected, however."],"answer":0,"explanation":"However used as a sentence adverbial at the beginning is followed by a comma.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-033","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In 'What a witness said surprised the jury', the clause 'What the witness said' functions as a:","options":["noun clause","adverbial clause","adjectival clause","prepositional phrase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The clause functions as the subject of 'surprised'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to \"scarce\" is","options":["abundant","limited","rare","meagre"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “abundant” because it is the closest opposite in meaning to “scarce”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-035","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression \"to throw in the towel\" means to","options":["give up","start again","conceal evidence","speak angrily"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The expression “to throw in the towel” means “give up” in standard English usage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the word nearest in meaning to \"obdurate\"","options":["yielding","stubborn","uncertain","cheerful"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “stubborn” because it is the closest synonym of “obdurate” in the intended context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-037","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Hardly had the meeting begun ____ the lights went out","options":["when","than","that","while"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “when” because the standard correlative construction is “hardly/scarcely … when”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-038","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The committee members agreed that the proposal was","options":["tentative","unacceptable","indispensable","ambiguous"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The correct answer is “indispensable” because it best fits the meaning and grammatical structure required by the sentence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-039","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P01","passage":"A community library is more than a room filled with books. When it is thoughtfully managed, it gives children access to stories, reference works and digital resources that may not be available at home. It also provides a quiet place for study. Yet a library does not become useful merely because shelves are full. Readers need guidance, relevant materials and programmes that encourage regular use. In one town, volunteers organised reading circles and invited local writers to speak. Attendance increased because the library became a place where people could discuss ideas, not simply borrow books. The experience suggests that access to information is strongest when it is combined with opportunities to use and share knowledge.","q":"Which of the following can be reasonably inferred from the passage?","options":["the library became socially useful","The topic has no limitations","One factor alone explains the entire issue","The writer rejects practical evidence"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The inference follows from the passage's emphasis and qualifications.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-040","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P04","passage":"Efficient public transport can reduce congestion, but adding buses alone does not guarantee improvement. If routes are poorly planned, vehicles may remain underused on some roads while overcrowding others. Reliability also matters: passengers are more willing to leave private cars when they can predict journey times. Safety, convenient stops and clear information contribute to that confidence. Consequently, transport planning is not simply a matter of increasing the number of vehicles. It requires attention to how the entire system works together.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'reliability' is closest in meaning to:","options":["reliability","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'reliability' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-use-of-english-2","title":"Use of English UTME Standard CBT 2","subject":"Use of English","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-041","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P09","passage":"Digital note-taking can be fast, but speed is not identical to learning. A student who records every sentence may produce a detailed transcript while processing very little. By contrast, selecting key ideas, reorganising them and adding brief questions can require slower but deeper thinking. Digital tools can support this process if they are used deliberately. Their value depends less on the device than on the mental activity it encourages.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'deliberately' is closest in meaning to:","options":["deliberately","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'deliberately' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-042","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P14","passage":"A science exhibition can reward spectacular displays, but spectacle is not the same as scientific quality. A modest project with a clear question, careful method and honest discussion of limitations may be more valuable than an elaborate demonstration that cannot be tested. Visitors may remember the colourful display, yet the educational purpose is to show how evidence is gathered and interpreted. Good exhibitions therefore make the reasoning behind a result visible.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'spectacular' is closest in meaning to:","options":["spectacular","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'spectacular' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-043","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P19","passage":"Volunteering is often praised for its social benefits, but it can also produce unintended effects when outside volunteers design projects without local participation. A well-intentioned programme may duplicate existing services or introduce solutions that do not fit local priorities. Effective volunteering therefore requires listening, collaboration and respect for local knowledge. The aim should not be to make volunteers the centre of a project but to strengthen the capacity of the community involved.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'duplicate' is closest in meaning to:","options":["duplicate","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'duplicate' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-044","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Select the sentence with the most appropriate formal register.","options":["The committee sort of messed up the timetable.","The committee has reviewed the timetable and identified the discrepancies.","The committee totally blew the timetable.","The committee did a thing with the timetable."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second sentence is precise, formal and appropriate for academic communication.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-045","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence 'The lecturer, as well as the students, was present', why is 'was' correct?","options":["The plural noun 'students' controls the verb.","The phrase 'as well as the students' is parenthetical and does not change the singular subject.","'Students' is an object complement.","The verb must always follow the last noun."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The grammatical subject is 'lecturer'; 'as well as the students' is an additional parenthetical phrase.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-046","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> I. The number of applicants has increased.<br><b>II.</b> II. A number of applicants has withdrawn.<br><b>III.</b> III. A number of applicants have withdrawn.<br><b>IV.</b> IV. The number of applicants have increased.","options":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'The number' is singular; 'a number of' takes a plural verb.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-047","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Select the word that is most opposite in meaning to 'meticulous'.","options":["careful","precise","slapdash","thorough"],"answer":2,"explanation":"'Slapdash' means careless or hurried and is the clearest antonym.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-048","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes: 'Hardly had the meeting begun ____ the electricity failed.'","options":["than","when","while","and"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fixed correlative construction is 'hardly...when'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-049","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence is logically free from ambiguity?","options":["I told the student that he had failed his brother.","After the teacher met the parent, she explained the result.","The principal informed the teacher that the report was inaccurate.","When the doctor spoke to the nurse, she was worried."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The third sentence clearly identifies the principal as the person who informed the teacher.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-050","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes: The lecturer insisted ____ seeing the original document.","options":["on","at","for","with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct collocation is 'insisted on'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-051","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In 'What the witness said surprised the jury', the clause 'What the witness said' functions as a:","options":["noun clause","adverbial clause","adjectival clause","prepositional phrase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The clause functions as the subject of 'surprised'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-052","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The board concluded that the new policy was","options":["redundant","indispensable","temporary","erratic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “indispensable” because it best fits the meaning and grammatical structure required by the sentence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-053","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Had the witness arrived earlier, the police","options":["would have questioned him","will question him","would question him yesterday","have questioned him"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “would have questioned him” because it supplies the grammatical form required to complete the sentence correctly.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-054","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression \"to read between the lines\" means to","options":["infer an unstated meaning","read aloud quickly","skip difficult words","memorise a passage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The expression “to read between the lines” means “infer an unstated meaning” in standard English usage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-055","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence \"What the witness reported surprised the panel\", the clause \"What the witness reported\" functions as a","options":["noun clause","adverbial clause","relative adjective clause","prepositional phrase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “noun clause” because the clause performs a noun function within the sentence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-056","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Scarcely had the rain stopped ____ the children returned outside","options":["when","than","that","since"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “when” because the standard correlative construction is “hardly/scarcely … when”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-057","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P01","passage":"A community library is more than a room filled with books. When it is thoughtfully managed, it gives children access to stories, reference works and digital resources that may not be available at home. It also provides a quiet place for study. Yet a library does not become useful merely because shelves are full. Readers need guidance, relevant materials and programmes that encourage regular use. In one town, volunteers organised reading circles and invited local writers to speak. Attendance increased because the library became a place where people could discuss ideas, not simply borrow books. The experience suggests that access to information is strongest when it is combined with opportunities to use and share knowledge.","q":"The writer's attitude to the subject can best be described as:","options":["constructive","hostile","indifferent","dismissive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The tone is best captured as constructive.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-058","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P05","passage":"Food labels are useful only when consumers can interpret them correctly. A list of ingredients tells what a product contains, while the nutrition panel gives information about quantities of nutrients and energy. Confusion may arise when attention is focused on one attractive claim, such as 'low fat', without considering serving size or added sugar. A careful reader therefore compares several parts of the label rather than treating a single phrase as a complete description of the product. In this sense, label reading is a small but important exercise in critical thinking.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'attractive' is closest in meaning to:","options":["attractive","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'attractive' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-059","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P10","passage":"Sleep is sometimes treated as time lost from study, yet learning does not end when a student closes a book. During sleep, the brain continues processes associated with memory and recovery. This does not mean that sleeping can replace preparation. A student who has not studied cannot expect sleep to create missing knowledge. Rather, adequate sleep may help the brain organise and retain what has already been learned. Effective study therefore involves not only what happens at the desk but also what happens after the desk is left.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'adequate' is closest in meaning to:","options":["adequate","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'adequate' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-060","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P15","passage":"Languages change because speakers adapt them to new situations, technologies and social relationships. Some people regard every new expression as evidence of decline, but change itself is not proof of deterioration. At the same time, not every innovation becomes permanent; expressions can disappear as quickly as they arise. The important question is therefore not whether a language changes, but how and why particular changes spread.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'deterioration' is closest in meaning to:","options":["deterioration","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'deterioration' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-061","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P20","passage":"Effective examination preparation is not measured by the number of hours spent at a desk alone. Practice should reveal weaknesses, while review should explain why an answer was wrong. Varying topics can also reduce the temptation to rely on a familiar pattern. A student who repeatedly answers easy questions may feel productive without addressing difficult areas. Preparation is strongest when it combines retrieval, correction, deliberate practice and realistic timing.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'retrieval' is closest in meaning to:","options":["retrieval","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'retrieval' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-062","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Identify the underlined function of the clause in: 'What the witness said surprised the jury.'","options":["adverbial clause","relative clause","noun clause","coordinate clause"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The clause 'What the witness said' functions as the subject, so it is a noun clause.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-063","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> A. I am interested in learning languages.<br><b>II.</b> B. I am interested to learning languages.<br><b>III.</b> C. She insisted on paying the bill.<br><b>IV.</b> D. She insisted to pay the bill.","options":["I and III are correct.","I and IV are correct.","II and III are correct.","II and IV are correct."],"answer":0,"explanation":"'Interested in' and 'insisted on' take the forms shown in I and III.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-064","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> I. He speaks as though he knew the answer.<br><b>II.</b> II. He speaks as though he knows the answer.<br><b>III.</b> III. She would rather walk than wait.<br><b>IV.</b> IV. She would rather walking than wait.","options":["I and III only","I and IV only","II and III only","II and IV only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The constructions in I and III are grammatically well formed in the intended meanings.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-065","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the sentence with correct concord.","options":["Each of the students have submitted their form.","Each of the students has submitted his or her form.","Each of the students have submitted his form.","Each of the students are submitting their form."],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Each' is singular and takes 'has' in formal standard English.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-066","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which word best describes the writer's attitude in a passage that presents evidence cautiously and acknowledges limitations?","options":["dogmatic","measured","sarcastic","dismissive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A cautious, evidence-aware tone is measured rather than dogmatic or dismissive.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-067","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option best preserves the meaning of 'The evidence is insufficient to establish guilt'?","options":["The evidence proves innocence.","The evidence does not provide enough basis to establish guilt.","The evidence is irrelevant to the case.","The evidence establishes guilt beyond doubt."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insufficient evidence means the available evidence does not meet the required basis for establishing guilt.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-068","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the word nearest in meaning to 'parsimonious'.","options":["frugal","reckless","talkative","generous"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parsimonious means excessively unwilling to spend.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-069","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a committee ____ earlier, the dispute might have been avoided.","options":["acts","acted","had acted","has acted"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The third conditional uses the past perfect in the if-clause.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-070","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The review panel judged the revised procedure to be","options":["essential","incidental","opaque","obsolete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “essential” because it best fits the meaning and grammatical structure required by the sentence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-071","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: If the manager had acted promptly, the dispute","options":["might have been avoided","will be avoided","is avoided","would avoid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “might have been avoided” because it supplies the grammatical form required to complete the sentence correctly.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-072","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression \"to keep someone at arm’s length\" means to","options":["avoid close involvement","offer physical assistance","invite someone to lead","criticise someone publicly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The expression “to keep someone at arm’s length” means “avoid close involvement” in standard English usage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-073","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence \"Although the road was flooded, the driver continued\", the clause \"Although the road was flooded\" is a","options":["adverbial clause","noun clause","relative clause","main clause"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “adverbial clause” because the clause modifies the main clause by expressing a circumstance such as time, reason or condition.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-074","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The lecturer insisted ____ seeing the original document","options":["on","at","for","with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “on” because it supplies the grammatical form required to complete the sentence correctly.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-075","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P01","passage":"A community library is more than a room filled with books. When it is thoughtfully managed, it gives children access to stories, reference works and digital resources that may not be available at home. It also provides a quiet place for study. Yet a library does not become useful merely because shelves are full. Readers need guidance, relevant materials and programmes that encourage regular use. In one town, volunteers organised reading circles and invited local writers to speak. Attendance increased because the library became a place where people could discuss ideas, not simply borrow books. The experience suggests that access to information is strongest when it is combined with opportunities to use and share knowledge.","q":"Which statement is supported by the passage?","options":["the library became socially useful","The opposite is always true","No conditions affect the issue","The topic is completely irrelevant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The passage directly supports the selected statement.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-076","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P06","passage":"Trees in cities provide shade, but their contribution extends beyond making streets pleasant. Their leaves can intercept some airborne particles, while their roots can help soil absorb rainwater. Trees may also reduce the heat stored by paved surfaces. Nevertheless, planting without planning can create problems: roots may damage infrastructure, and unsuitable species may require excessive maintenance. Urban tree programmes are therefore most effective when species, location and long-term care are considered together.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'unsuitable' is closest in meaning to:","options":["unsuitable","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'unsuitable' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-077","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P11","passage":"A repair culture challenges the assumption that damaged objects should immediately be discarded. Repairing a chair, appliance or garment can extend its useful life and reduce waste. Yet repair is not always cheaper than replacement, particularly when spare parts are unavailable or labour is expensive. The strongest argument for repair is therefore not simply that it always saves money. It is that repair can preserve value, skills and materials when conditions make it practical.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'discarded' is closest in meaning to:","options":["discarded","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'discarded' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-078","type":"passage","passageId":"ENG-P16","passage":"Improving school attendance is sometimes approached by simply urging families to send children to school more often. Encouragement matters, but attendance can also be affected by transport, illness, school climate, household responsibilities and perceived relevance of lessons. If these barriers are ignored, repeated warnings may produce little change. Effective attendance strategies therefore begin by identifying the causes of absence rather than assuming that every absence has the same explanation.","q":"As used in the passage, the word 'perceived' is closest in meaning to:","options":["perceived","ordinary","uncertain","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In context, 'perceived' carries its contextual meaning as stated in the passage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-079","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that correctly punctuates the sentence.","options":["However, the evidence was incomplete.","However; the evidence was incomplete.","However: the evidence was incomplete.","However the evidence was incomplete."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When however introduces a contrast at the beginning of a sentence, it is normally followed by a comma.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-080","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which of the following statements are grammatically correct?<br><br><b>I.</b> I. The news is encouraging.<br><b>II.</b> II. The news are encouraging.<br><b>III.</b> III. Mathematics is difficult for some learners.<br><b>IV.</b> IV. Mathematics are difficult for some learners.","options":["I and III only","II and IV only","I, II and III only","II, III and IV only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'News' and the school subject 'Mathematics' are grammatically singular in these contexts.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-use-of-english-3","title":"Use of English UTME Standard CBT 3","subject":"Use of English","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-081","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Read the sentence: 'Although the proposal was expensive, the board approved it because the projected benefits were substantial.' What is the main logical relationship?","options":["contrast followed by reason","cause followed by contrast","sequence followed by comparison","definition followed by conclusion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'Although' introduces a concession/contrast, while 'because' introduces the reason for approval.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-082","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a formal report, which transition best introduces a consequence?","options":["Nevertheless","Consequently","Conversely","Similarly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"'Consequently' signals a result or consequence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-083","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the correctly transformed sentence: 'The teacher said, \"I will submit the report tomorrow.\"'","options":["The teacher said that she would submit the report the next day.","The teacher said that I would submit the report tomorrow.","The teacher says that she would submit the report yesterday.","The teacher said she will submit the report the next day."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Backshift changes 'will' to 'would' and 'tomorrow' to 'the next day' in reported speech.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-084","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence uses 'affect' correctly as a verb?","options":["The new timetable may affect attendance.","The new timetable may effect attendance only as a noun.","The new timetable may affects attendance.","The new timetable may affecting attendance."],"answer":0,"explanation":"As a verb, 'affect' means to influence; 'may' is followed by the base form 'affect'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz Original Advanced Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-085","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes: Hardly had the meeting begun ____ the lights went out.","options":["when","than","then","while"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The standard correlative construction is 'hardly...when'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-086","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose a option that best completes: The lecturer insisted ____ seeing the original document.","options":["on","at","for","with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct collocation is 'insisted on'.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-087","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to \"transient\" is","options":["permanent","brief","fragile","uncertain"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “permanent” because it is the closest opposite in meaning to “transient”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-088","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: If she had studied the instructions carefully, she","options":["would not have made the error","will not make the error","does not make the error","has not made the error"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “would not have made the error” because a third conditional uses past perfect in the if-clause and would have + past participle in the result clause.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-089","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the word nearest in meaning to \"circumspect\"","options":["careless","cautious","hostile","generous"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “cautious” because it is the closest synonym of “circumspect” in the intended context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-090","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence \"The student who solved the problem received a prize\", the clause \"who solved the problem\" is a","options":["relative clause","noun clause","adverbial clause","main clause"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “relative clause” because the clause modifies a preceding noun and is introduced by a relative pronoun.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-091","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The candidate refrained ____ commenting on the allegation","options":["from","with","at","by"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The correct answer is “from” because the standard construction is “refrain from” followed by a noun or gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-092","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Neither the lecturer nor the students ____ aware of the change in venue.","options":["was","were","has been","is"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The nearer subject, “students”, is plural; therefore the plural verb “were” agrees with it.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-093","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: By the time the ambulance arrived, the injured cyclist ____ conscious.","options":["has regained","had regained","will regain","is regaining"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The past perfect “had regained” shows that the recovery occurred before another past event.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-094","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The principal insisted that every candidate ____ the examination rules.","options":["reads","read","reading","has read"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After “insisted that” in a formal subjunctive construction, the base form “read” is used.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-095","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Hardly ____ the announcement when the crowd began to cheer.","options":["had she finished","she had finished","did she finish","she finished"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Hardly … when” takes inversion after “hardly”; “had she finished” is the required past-perfect form.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-096","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The committee has been meeting weekly ____ January.","options":["for","since","during","by"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Since” introduces the point in time from which an action has continued.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-097","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: If Musa were more careful, he ____ so many errors.","options":["would not make","will not make","would not have made","did not make"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is a second conditional: “if” + past form, followed by “would” + base verb.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-098","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The documents, together with the receipt, ____ in the drawer.","options":["is","are","were being","have"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “are” because the main subject “documents” is plural; “together with the receipt” is an additional phrase and does not change the number of the subject.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-099","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: She is one of the researchers who ____ published their findings this year.","options":["has","have","is","was"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The relative pronoun “who” refers to plural “researchers”, so “have” is required.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-100","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The manager asked whether the suppliers ____ the revised quotation.","options":["receive","had received","will receive","are receiving"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The reported question refers to an action completed before the past reporting verb, so past perfect is appropriate.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-101","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The witness spoke so quietly that nobody could ____ what he said.","options":["make out","make up","make over","make for"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Make out” means to perceive or understand something that is difficult to hear or see.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-102","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: It is high time the authorities ____ the drainage system.","options":["repair","repaired","will repair","have repaired"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“It is high time” is conventionally followed by a past-tense form for a present or future desired action.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-103","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: No candidate is allowed to leave the hall ____ the chief invigilator gives permission.","options":["unless","because","despite","whereas"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Unless” introduces the condition under which leaving is permitted.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-104","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The new policy is intended to prevent students ____ prohibited materials into the hall.","options":["bring","to bringing","from bringing","for bringing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The standard construction is “prevent someone from doing something”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-105","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The scientist succeeded ____ isolating the compound.","options":["at","in","on","with"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard collocation is “succeed in” followed by a noun or gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-106","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The chairman, as well as his deputies, ____ expected to attend.","options":["are","were","is","have"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The subject is “chairman”; “as well as his deputies” is an added phrase, so the singular verb “is” agrees with the subject.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-107","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The more carefully you analyse the data, the ____ your conclusion will be.","options":["accurate","more accurate","most accurate","accurately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correlative comparative structure is “the more …, the more …”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-108","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: She would rather ____ at home than attend the noisy gathering.","options":["stays","stayed","stay","staying"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Would rather” is followed by the base form of the verb when the subject refers to the present preference.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-109","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The report is believed ____ by an independent panel.","options":["to review","to have reviewed","to have been reviewed","reviewing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The passive infinitive “to have been reviewed” shows that the report underwent the review.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-110","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: He denied ____ the confidential file.","options":["to open","opening","to have open","open"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Deny” is followed by a gerund, hence “opening”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-111","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The lecturer advised the students ____ their calculations before submitting the scripts.","options":["check","checking","to check","checked"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Advise someone to do something” requires the infinitive “to check”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-112","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The equipment must be switched off before the technician ____ it.","options":["examines","will examine","examined","has examined"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a time clause introduced by “before”, the simple present can refer to a future event.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-113","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: Had the driver obeyed the warning, the accident ____ avoided.","options":["will be","would be","would have been","has been"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A third conditional uses “would have + past participle” for the unreal past result.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-114","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The lecturer gave the students ____ information that they could not finish copying it.","options":["so many","such much","so much","such many"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Information” is uncountable, so “so much information” is correct.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-115","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: There were ____ applicants that the interview panel worked late.","options":["so much","such many","so many","too much"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Applicants” is countable plural, so “so many” is the correct quantifier.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-116","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The two witnesses gave accounts that were completely at ____ with each other.","options":["variance","variance to","difference","odds"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The idiomatic expression is “at odds with”, meaning in disagreement.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-117","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The evidence was insufficient to ____ the accused of the charge.","options":["convict","convince","confirm","convince with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Convict” means to find someone guilty of an offence; “convince” does not take this legal object in the same way.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-118","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The students were warned not to tamper ____ the laboratory equipment.","options":["on","with","at","for"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard collocation is “tamper with” something.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-119","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The committee arrived ____ a unanimous decision after lengthy deliberations.","options":["at","on","to","with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The standard expression is “arrive at a decision”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-120","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The candidate was disqualified ____ submitting two answer booklets.","options":["by","for","with","at"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Disqualified for” introduces the reason for disqualification.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-use-of-english-4","title":"Use of English UTME Standard CBT 4","subject":"Use of English","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-121","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The professor is capable ____ explaining complex ideas in simple language.","options":["to","for","of","with"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The adjective “capable” is followed by “of” and a gerund or noun.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-122","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “meticulous” is","options":["careless","precise","thorough","systematic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Careless” is the closest antonym of “meticulous”, which means very careful and exact.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-123","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “lucid” is","options":["obscure","clear","hostile","lengthy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Lucid” means clear and easy to understand.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “frugal” is","options":["economical","wasteful","thrifty","prudent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Wasteful” is the closest antonym of “frugal”, which describes careful use of money or resources.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-125","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “resilient” is","options":["fragile","adaptable","rigid","inactive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Resilient” describes something able to recover or adapt after difficulty.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-126","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “obsolete” is","options":["outdated","current","discarded","ancient"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Current” is the closest opposite of “obsolete”, which means no longer in use.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-127","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “tenacious” is","options":["persistent","hesitant","indifferent","careless"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Tenacious” means persistent and unwilling to give up.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-128","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “benevolent” is","options":["kind","charitable","malevolent","generous"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Malevolent” is the opposite of “benevolent”: it denotes ill will rather than goodwill.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-129","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “scrutinize” is","options":["ignore","examine closely","summarize","approve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To “scrutinize” something is to examine it very carefully.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-130","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “concise” is","options":["brief","precise","verbose","clear"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Verbose” is the closest antonym of “concise”, meaning expressed in few words.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-131","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “ubiquitous” is","options":["rare","widespread","hidden","temporary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Ubiquitous” means present or found everywhere.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-132","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “hostile” is","options":["friendly","aggressive","unfriendly","violent"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Friendly” is the closest antonym of “hostile”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-133","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “pragmatic” is","options":["practical","theoretical","emotional","idealistic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Pragmatic” means practical and focused on workable results.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-134","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “transient” is","options":["brief","permanent","temporary","fleeting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Permanent” is the opposite of “transient”, which means short-lived.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-135","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “candid” is","options":["evasive","frank","secretive","ambiguous"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Candid” means frank, open and honest.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-136","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “diligent” is","options":["industrious","careless","assiduous","conscientious"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Careless” is the closest antonym of “diligent”, which means hardworking and careful.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-137","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “ambiguous” is","options":["unclear","certain","obvious","definite"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Ambiguous” describes something open to more than one interpretation or unclear.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-138","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “scarce” is","options":["limited","abundant","rare","insufficient"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Abundant” is the closest antonym of “scarce”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-139","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “arduous” is","options":["easy","difficult","pleasant","brief"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Arduous” means requiring considerable effort or difficult to accomplish.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-140","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “transparency” is","options":["openness","clarity","secrecy","honesty"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Secrecy” is the closest opposite of transparency in the sense of openness.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-141","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “inadvertent” is","options":["deliberate","accidental","careful","planned"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Inadvertent” means unintentional or accidental.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-142","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “coherent” is","options":["logical","consistent","disjointed","orderly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Disjointed” is the closest antonym of “coherent”, which means logically connected.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-143","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “substantiate” is","options":["contradict","support with evidence","conceal","simplify"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To “substantiate” a claim is to support it with evidence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-144","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “mandatory” is","options":["compulsory","optional","required","binding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Optional” is the opposite of “mandatory”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-145","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “sporadic” is","options":["regular","occasional","continuous","constant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Sporadic” means occurring irregularly or occasionally.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-146","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “hostile” in a diplomatic context is","options":["conciliatory","aggressive","belligerent","antagonistic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Conciliatory” means intended to restore friendly relations, making it the opposite of “hostile”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-147","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “alleviate” is","options":["worsen","relieve","prolong","conceal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To “alleviate” a problem is to make it less severe or painful.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-148","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “rigid” is","options":["strict","inflexible","flexible","fixed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Flexible” is the closest antonym of “rigid”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-149","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “pervasive” is","options":["widespread","isolated","insignificant","temporary"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Pervasive” means spreading widely throughout an area or group.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-150","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word most nearly opposite in meaning to “reticent” is","options":["reserved","talkative","quiet","reluctant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Talkative” is the closest opposite of “reticent”, which describes someone reluctant to speak.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-151","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The word nearest in meaning to “disparage” is","options":["praise","belittle","defend","imitate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To “disparage” someone or something is to belittle or speak of it disrespectfully.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-152","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to bite the bullet” means to","options":["avoid responsibility","face a difficult situation bravely","celebrate unexpectedly","change one’s opinion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“To bite the bullet” means to face something difficult or unpleasant with courage.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-153","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to call it a day” means to","options":["begin work early","stop working for the time being","criticize a colleague","announce a holiday"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“To call it a day” means to stop an activity, especially work, for the present.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-154","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to be in hot water” means to","options":["be physically uncomfortable","be in trouble","be extremely successful","be very thirsty"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“To be in hot water” means to be in difficulty or trouble.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-155","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to spill the beans” means to","options":["waste food","reveal a secret","make a false accusation","leave suddenly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“To spill the beans” means to disclose information that was meant to remain secret.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-156","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to cut corners” means to","options":["shorten a road","do something cheaply or carelessly by omitting necessary steps","decorate a building","reduce a price honestly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“To cut corners” means to take shortcuts by omitting proper or necessary steps.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-157","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to hit the nail on the head” means to","options":["miss the point","identify the exact issue","work with tools","make a careless error"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression means to identify or state something exactly.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-158","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to keep something at bay” means to","options":["keep it nearby","prevent it from approaching or causing harm","hide it completely","increase its influence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Keep at bay” means to hold something harmful or unwanted at a distance.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-159","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to go the extra mile” means to","options":["travel unnecessarily","make an additional effort","avoid responsibility","finish immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Go the extra mile” means to make more effort than is normally expected.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-160","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to read between the lines” means to","options":["read very quickly","look for an implied meaning","skip difficult words","memorize a passage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression means to infer a meaning that is not directly stated.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-use-of-english-5","title":"Use of English UTME Standard CBT 5","subject":"Use of English","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-161","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to turn a blind eye” means to","options":["lose one’s sight","deliberately ignore something wrong","observe carefully","change direction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Turn a blind eye” means to deliberately ignore a problem or wrongdoing.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-162","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to weather the storm” means to","options":["predict rainfall","survive a difficult period","create confusion","avoid travel"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression means to endure and survive a difficult situation.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-163","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to throw cold water on” an idea means to","options":["support it strongly","discourage or criticize it","make it public","develop it further"],"answer":1,"explanation":"To throw cold water on an idea is to discourage enthusiasm for it.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-164","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to take something with a pinch of salt” means to","options":["eat carefully","treat information with some scepticism","accept every claim","ignore all evidence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression means not to accept a statement as completely true without questioning it.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-165","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to have a change of heart” means to","options":["become physically ill","change one’s attitude or decision","forget a name","become confused"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A change of heart is a change in attitude, feeling or decision.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-166","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"The expression “to put the cart before the horse” means to","options":["work efficiently","do things in the wrong order","travel by road","avoid planning"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression means to reverse the proper order of actions.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-167","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a formal letter, the most appropriate opening is","options":["Hey there,","Dear Sir or Madam,","Hi guys,","What’s up?"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Dear Sir or Madam” is an appropriately formal salutation when the recipient’s name is unknown.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-168","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In an official memorandum, the most appropriate closing is","options":["Cheers!","Yours faithfully,","See you soon,","Lots of love,"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Yours faithfully” is a formal closing suitable for official correspondence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-169","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which expression is most appropriate in a formal academic report?","options":["The results were kinda surprising.","The results were somewhat unexpected.","The results were super amazing.","The results were pretty wild."],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Somewhat unexpected” is appropriately formal and precise for an academic report.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-170","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which word is most appropriate in a formal report to replace “get” in “The researchers got the data”?","options":["grab","obtained","snatched","picked"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Obtained” is the formal and precise verb for acquiring data.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-171","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option is most suitable for a formal complaint?","options":["You people messed everything up.","I wish to formally report the issue outlined below.","You guys are terrible.","Fix this thing now!"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second option is polite, precise and appropriately formal for a written complaint.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-172","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option is most suitable for a formal academic conclusion?","options":["Anyway, that’s all.","In conclusion, the findings support the proposed explanation.","So, yeah, it worked.","That’s basically it."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second option uses formal academic language and clearly states the conclusion.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-173","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which word best replaces “a lot of” in formal academic writing?","options":["loads of","numerous","tons of","heaps of"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Numerous” is a concise formal alternative to “a lot of” when referring to countable nouns.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-174","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option best expresses disagreement politely in an academic discussion?","options":["You are completely wrong.","The evidence does not appear to support that conclusion.","That idea is nonsense.","You clearly do not understand."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second option challenges the conclusion by referring to evidence rather than attacking the person.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-175","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option is most appropriate for a formal request?","options":["Send me the file now.","I would be grateful if you could forward the file.","Give me the file.","I need that file ASAP."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The second option uses a polite, formal request structure.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-176","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which word best completes the formal expression: “Please find the documents ____ for your consideration.”","options":["attached","attachedly","attaching","attach"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Please find the documents attached” is a standard formal expression.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-177","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence “What the witness reported surprised the panel”, the clause “What the witness reported” functions as a","options":["noun clause","relative clause","adverbial clause","prepositional phrase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The clause functions as the subject of “surprised”, so it performs a noun function.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-178","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence “Although the road was flooded, the driver continued”, the clause “Although the road was flooded” is a","options":["noun clause","adverbial clause","relative clause","main clause"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The clause modifies the main clause by expressing a concession, so it is adverbial.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-179","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In the sentence “The student who solved the problem received a prize”, the clause “who solved the problem” is a","options":["relative clause","noun clause","adverbial clause","main clause"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The clause modifies the noun “student” and begins with the relative pronoun “who”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-180","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option contains a correctly formed question tag?","options":["You are coming, are you?","You are coming, aren’t you?","You are coming, isn’t it?","You are coming, won’t you?"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A positive statement normally takes a negative tag using the same auxiliary: “aren’t you?”","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-181","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence correctly uses the determiner “each”?","options":["Each of the players have a locker.","Each of the players has a locker.","Each players has a locker.","Each of players have a locker."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “Each of the players has a locker” because “each” is singular and therefore takes the singular verb “has”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-182","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence correctly uses the uncountable noun “news”?","options":["The news are encouraging.","The news is encouraging.","The news have been encouraging.","The news were encouraging."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “The news is encouraging” because “news” is an uncountable singular noun and takes a singular verb.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-183","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence correctly uses the name of the subject “Mathematics”?","options":["Mathematics are my strongest subject.","Mathematics is my strongest subject.","Mathematics have been my strongest subject.","Mathematics were my strongest subject."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “Mathematics is my strongest subject” because the name of the academic subject takes a singular verb.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-184","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which sentence correctly uses the uncountable noun “furniture”?","options":["The furniture were delivered yesterday.","The furniture was delivered yesterday.","The furnitures were delivered yesterday.","The furniture have delivered yesterday."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The correct answer is “The furniture was delivered yesterday” because “furniture” is an uncountable noun and takes a singular verb.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-185","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The principal said that the school ____ the new laboratory the following month.","options":["opens","would open","will open","has opened"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In reported speech after a past reporting verb, the future “will open” changes to “would open”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-186","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: I wish I ____ the answer to that question.","options":["know","knew","have known","will know"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Wish” about a present unreal situation commonly takes the simple past, “knew”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-187","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: If the committee ____ the evidence earlier, it would have reached a different conclusion.","options":["reviews","reviewed","had reviewed","has reviewed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A third conditional uses past perfect in the if-clause: “had reviewed”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-188","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The lecturer asked me ____ I had completed the assignment.","options":["that","whether","which","what"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Whether” introduces an indirect yes/no question.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-189","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The candidate was prevented ____ entering the examination hall.","options":["to","from","at","with"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard construction is “prevented from” followed by a gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-190","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: She apologized ____ arriving late.","options":["for","to","with","at"],"answer":0,"explanation":"“Apologize for” is followed by a noun or gerund describing the offence.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-191","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: He is responsible ____ preparing the final report.","options":["of","for","to","with"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard collocation is “responsible for”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-192","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The new findings are consistent ____ the earlier study.","options":["to","with","on","at"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard collocation is “consistent with”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-193","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The witness was reluctant ____ answer the question.","options":["answering","to answer","answer","answered"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Reluctant” is followed by the infinitive “to answer”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-194","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The committee is committed ____ improving the quality of instruction.","options":["to","for","at","with"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The expression “committed to” is followed by a noun or gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-195","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The lecturer objected ____ the proposal on ethical grounds.","options":["against","to","with","at"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard construction is “object to” something.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-196","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The students succeeded ____ completing the project on time.","options":["at","in","with","on"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Succeed in” is followed by a noun or gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-197","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The manager is accustomed ____ working under pressure.","options":["with","to","for","at"],"answer":1,"explanation":"“Accustomed to” is followed by a noun or gerund.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-198","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The doctor recommended that the patient ____ more water.","options":["drinks","drink","drank","drinking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After “recommended that”, the base form “drink” is appropriate in formal English.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-199","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: It was the assistant ____ discovered the error.","options":["which","whom","who","whose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Who” refers to the person performing the action “discovered”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false},{"id":"qb-use-of-english-final-200","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: The book ____ you lent me is on the desk.","options":["who","whom","which","whose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"“Which” is used as the relative pronoun referring to the thing “book”.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Practice Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Use of English","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-physics-1","title":"Physics UTME Standard CBT 1","subject":"Physics","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 2 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 5 Ω resistor carrying 4 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 20 V.\nII. The electrical power is 80 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 8 Hz and wavelength 0.29 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 7 kg object raised through 8 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 560 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 6 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 9 Ω resistor carrying 3 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 27 V.\nII. The electrical power is 81 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 12 Hz and wavelength 0.33 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 11 kg object raised through 5 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 550 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-01","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 3 kg of water by 6 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["75,600 J","12,600 J","25,200 J","7,560.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-02","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 340 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["5.850e-19 J","7.514e-49 J","2.925e-19 J","6.763e-32 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-03","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 10 m/s and uniform acceleration 4 m/s² for 5 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["100 m","50 m","150 m","Insufficient information to determine the answer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-04","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 4 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 20 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["5 m/s²","20 m/s²","4 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-05","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 5 kg is lifted vertically through 6 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["300 J","30 J","50 J","150 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-06","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 4 kg trolley increases its speed from 6 m/s to 13 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["28 N s","52 N s","24 N s","76 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-07","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 5 Ω carries a current of 4 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["80 W","20 W","9 W","1.25 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-08","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 7 Hz and wavelength 0.3 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["2.1 m/s","23.3333 m/s","7.3 m/s","0.0428571 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-09","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 3 kg object moves at 6 m/s in a circle of radius 4 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["27 N","4.5 N","72 N","432 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-10","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.14 m and forms a real image when an object is 0.4 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.215 m","0.104 m","0.260 m","0.056 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-11","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 4 kg of water by 7 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["117,600 J","16,800 J","29,400 J","11,760.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-12","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 360 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["5.525e-19 J","7.956e-49 J","2.762e-19 J","7.160e-32 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-13","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 11 m/s and uniform acceleration 5 m/s² for 6 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["156 m","66 m","246 m","90 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-14","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 5 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 30 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["6 m/s²","30 m/s²","5 m/s²","7 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-15","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 6 kg is lifted vertically through 7 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["420 J","42 J","60 J","210 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-16","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 5 kg trolley increases its speed from 7 m/s to 15 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["40 N s","75 N s","35 N s","110 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-17","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 6 Ω carries a current of 5 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["150 W","30 W","11 W","1.2 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-18","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 8 Hz and wavelength 0.35 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["2.8 m/s","22.8571 m/s","8.35 m/s","0.04375 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-19","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 4 kg object moves at 7 m/s in a circle of radius 5 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["39.2 N","5.6 N","140 N","980 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-20","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.16 m and forms a real image when an object is 0.45 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.248 m","0.118 m","0.290 m","0.072 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-21","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 5 kg of water by 8 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["168,000 J","21,000 J","33,600 J","16,800.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-22","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 380 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["5.234e-19 J","8.398e-49 J","2.617e-19 J","7.558e-32 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-23","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 12 m/s and uniform acceleration 6 m/s² for 7 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["231 m","84 m","378 m","147 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-24","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 6 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 42 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["7 m/s²","42 m/s²","6 m/s²","8 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-25","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 7 kg is lifted vertically through 8 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["560 J","56 J","70 J","280 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-26","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 6 kg trolley increases its speed from 8 m/s to 17 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["54 N s","102 N s","48 N s","150 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-27","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 7 Ω carries a current of 6 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["252 W","42 W","13 W","1.16667 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-28","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 9 Hz and wavelength 0.4 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["3.6 m/s","22.5 m/s","9.4 m/s","0.0444444 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-29","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 5 kg object moves at 8 m/s in a circle of radius 6 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["53.3333 N","6.66667 N","240 N","1920 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-30","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.18 m and forms a real image when an object is 0.5 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.281 m","0.132 m","0.320 m","0.090 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 6 kg of water by 9 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["226,800 J","25,200 J","37,800 J","22,680.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["4.972e-19 J","8.840e-49 J","2.486e-19 J","7.956e-32 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-physics-2","title":"Physics UTME Standard CBT 2","subject":"Physics","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 42 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 45 Ω resistor carrying 4 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 180 V.\nII. The electrical power is 720 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 48 Hz and wavelength 0.69 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 47 kg object raised through 6 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 2820 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 46 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 49 Ω resistor carrying 3 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 147 V.\nII. The electrical power is 441 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 52 Hz and wavelength 0.73 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 51 kg object raised through 3 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 1530 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 7 kg of water by 10 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["294,000 J","29,400 J","42,000 J","29,400.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 420 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["4.736e-19 J","9.282e-49 J","2.368e-19 J","8.354e-32 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 14 m/s and uniform acceleration 3 m/s² for 3 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["55.5 m","42 m","69 m","13.5 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 8 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 24 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["3 m/s²","24 m/s²","8 m/s²","4 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 9 kg is lifted vertically through 10 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["900 J","90 J","450 J","Insufficient information to determine the answer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 8 kg trolley increases its speed from 10 m/s to 16 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["48 N s","128 N s","80 N s","208 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 9 Ω carries a current of 8 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["576 W","72 W","17 W","1.125 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 11 Hz and wavelength 0.5 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["5.5 m/s","22 m/s","11.5 m/s","0.0454545 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 2 kg object moves at 10 m/s in a circle of radius 8 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["25 N","2.5 N","160 N","1600 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.22 m and forms a real image when an object is 0.6 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.347 m","0.161 m","0.380 m","0.132 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 8 kg of water by 11 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["369,600 J","33,600 J","46,200 J","36,960.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":nu