m/s² for 6 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["174 m","138 m","210 m","36 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-083","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 17 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 102 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["6 m/s²","102 m/s²","17 m/s²","7 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-084","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 18 kg is lifted vertically through 10 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["1800 J","180 J","900 J","Insufficient information to determine the answer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-085","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 17 kg trolley increases its speed from 19 m/s to 24 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["85 N s","408 N s","323 N s","731 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-086","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 10 Ω carries a current of 17 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["2890 W","170 W","27 W","0.588235 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-087","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 20 Hz and wavelength 0.55 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["11 m/s","36.3636 m/s","20.55 m/s","0.0275 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-088","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 1 kg object moves at 19 m/s in a circle of radius 17 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["21.2353 N","1.11765 N","323 N","6137 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-090","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.24 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.05 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.311 m","0.195 m","0.810 m","0.252 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-091","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 17 kg of water by 12 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["856,800 J","71,400 J","50,400 J","85,680.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-092","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 620 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["3.208e-19 J","1.370e-48 J","1.604e-19 J","1.233e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-093","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 24 m/s and uniform acceleration 3 m/s² for 7 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["241.5 m","168 m","315 m","73.5 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-094","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 18 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 126 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["7 m/s²","126 m/s²","18 m/s²","8 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-095","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 19 kg is lifted vertically through 11 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["2090 J","209 J","190 J","1045 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-096","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 18 kg trolley increases its speed from 20 m/s to 26 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["108 N s","468 N s","360 N s","828 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-097","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 3 Ω carries a current of 18 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["972 W","54 W","21 W","0.166667 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-098","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 21 Hz and wavelength 0.2 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["4.2 m/s","105 m/s","21.2 m/s","0.00952381 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-099","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 2 kg object moves at 20 m/s in a circle of radius 18 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["44.4444 N","2.22222 N","720 N","14400 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-100","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.1 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.1 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.110 m","0.092 m","1.000 m","Insufficient information to determine the answer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-101","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 18 kg of water by 5 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["378,000 J","75,600 J","21,000 J","37,800.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-102","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 640 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["3.108e-19 J","1.414e-48 J","1.554e-19 J","1.273e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-physics-5","title":"Physics UTME Standard CBT 5","subject":"Physics","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 162 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-037","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 165 Ω resistor carrying 4 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 660 V.\nII. The electrical power is 2640 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 168 Hz and wavelength 1.89 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-038","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 167 kg object raised through 7 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 11690 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-019","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 166 kg body moving on a straight track are correct?\n\nI. If the resultant force is non-zero, its velocity changes.\nII. If the resultant force is zero, the body may still move at constant velocity.\nIII. For a given mass, doubling the resultant force doubles the acceleration.\nIV. Acceleration is measured in newtons.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; acceleration is measured in m/s², while the newton is a unit of force.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-039","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a circuit containing a 169 Ω resistor carrying 3 A are correct?\n\nI. The potential difference across the resistor is 507 V.\nII. The electrical power is 1521 W.\nIII. Ohm’s law gives V=IR for an ohmic resistor at constant physical conditions.\nIV. The resistance is found by multiplying voltage by current.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; resistance is R=V/I, not VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-020","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a wave of frequency 172 Hz and wavelength 1.93 m are correct?\n\nI. Its speed is fλ.\nII. Its period is 1/f.\nIII. If frequency increases while wavelength remains fixed, speed increases.\nIV. Frequency is measured in metres.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; frequency is measured in hertz.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-physics-roman-clean-040","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a 171 kg object raised through 4 m are correct, taking g=10 m/s²?\n\nI. Its gravitational potential energy increases by mgh.\nII. The increase is 6840 J.\nIII. Raising it higher increases its gravitational potential energy.\nIV. Gravitational potential energy is measured in watts.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; energy is measured in joules, while the watt is a unit of power.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":true},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-103","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 19 kg of water by 6 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["478,800 J","79,800 J","25,200 J","47,880.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-104","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 660 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["3.014e-19 J","1.459e-48 J","1.507e-19 J","1.313e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-105","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 26 m/s and uniform acceleration 5 m/s² for 3 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["100.5 m","78 m","123 m","22.5 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-107","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 20 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 60 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["3 m/s²","60 m/s²","20 m/s²","4 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-108","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 21 kg is lifted vertically through 4 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["840 J","84 J","210 J","420 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-109","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 20 kg trolley increases its speed from 22 m/s to 30 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["160 N s","600 N s","440 N s","1040 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-110","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 5 Ω carries a current of 20 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["2000 W","100 W","25 W","0.25 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-111","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 23 Hz and wavelength 0.3 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["6.9 m/s","76.6667 m/s","23.3 m/s","0.0130435 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-112","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 4 kg object moves at 22 m/s in a circle of radius 20 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["96.8 N","4.4 N","1760 N","38720 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-113","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.14 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.2 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.158 m","0.125 m","1.060 m","0.168 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-114","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 20 kg of water by 7 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["588,000 J","84,000 J","29,400 J","58,800.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-115","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 680 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["2.925e-19 J","1.503e-48 J","1.462e-19 J","1.353e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-116","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 27 m/s and uniform acceleration 6 m/s² for 4 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["156 m","108 m","204 m","48 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-117","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 21 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 84 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["4 m/s²","84 m/s²","21 m/s²","5 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-118","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 22 kg is lifted vertically through 5 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["1100 J","110 J","220 J","550 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-119","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 21 kg trolley increases its speed from 23 m/s to 32 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["189 N s","672 N s","483 N s","1155 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-120","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 6 Ω carries a current of 21 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["2646 W","126 W","27 W","0.285714 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-121","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 24 Hz and wavelength 0.35 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["8.4 m/s","68.5714 m/s","24.35 m/s","0.0145833 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-122","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 5 kg object moves at 23 m/s in a circle of radius 21 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["125.952 N","5.47619 N","2415 N","55545 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-123","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.16 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.25 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.183 m","0.142 m","1.090 m","0.200 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 21 kg of water by 8 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["705,600 J","88,200 J","33,600 J","70,560.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-125","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 700 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["2.841e-19 J","1.547e-48 J","1.421e-19 J","1.392e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-126","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 28 m/s and uniform acceleration 2 m/s² for 5 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["165 m","140 m","190 m","25 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-127","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 22 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 110 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["5 m/s²","110 m/s²","22 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-128","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 23 kg is lifted vertically through 6 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["1380 J","138 J","230 J","690 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-129","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 22 kg trolley increases its speed from 24 m/s to 29 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["110 N s","638 N s","528 N s","1166 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-130","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 7 Ω carries a current of 22 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["3388 W","154 W","29 W","0.318182 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1000","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 25 Hz and wavelength 0.4 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["10 m/s","62.5 m/s","25.4 m/s","0.016 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 1 kg object moves at 24 m/s in a circle of radius 22 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["26.1818 N","1.09091 N","528 N","12672 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.18 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.3 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.209 m","0.158 m","1.120 m","0.234 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 22 kg of water by 9 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["831,600 J","92,400 J","37,800 J","83,160.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 720 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["2.762e-19 J","1.591e-48 J","1.381e-19 J","1.432e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-chemistry-1","title":"Chemistry UTME Standard CBT 1","subject":"Chemistry","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 10 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 10 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 10.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 9 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.06 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.06 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 3 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 9 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C9H20.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 14 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 14 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 14.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 13 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.14 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.14 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 7 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 13 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C13H28.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-01","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 3 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0056 mol","540 mol","0.0112 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-02","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 311 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-03","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 14 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.318 mol","616.0 mol","3.143 mol","14.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-04","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.14 dm³ of a 0.07 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.392 g","2.800 g","5.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-05","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 6 dm³ at 4 atm. What volume will it occupy at 3 atm at constant temperature?","options":["8 dm³","4.5 dm³","18 dm³","7 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-06","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 6 carbon atoms?","options":["C6H14","C6H12","C6H10","C7H14"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-07","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 8 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["5","8","11","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-08","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.003 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.52","3.52","11.48","1.52"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-09","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 48 g carbon, 8 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C1H3O1","C2H3O1","C1H4O1","C1H3O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-10","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in SO₄²⁻?","options":["+6","+4","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-11","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 4 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0099 mol","960 mol","0.0199 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-12","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 322 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-13","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 15 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.341 mol","660.0 mol","2.933 mol","15.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-14","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.16 dm³ of a 0.08 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.512 g","3.200 g","6.400 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-15","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 7 dm³ at 5 atm. What volume will it occupy at 4 atm at constant temperature?","options":["8.75 dm³","5.6 dm³","28 dm³","8 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-16","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 7 carbon atoms?","options":["C7H16","C7H14","C7H12","C8H16"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-17","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 9 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["8","9","10","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-18","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.004 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.40","3.40","11.60","1.40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-19","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 60 g carbon, 10 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C5H10O1","C6H10O1","C5H11O1","C5H10O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-20","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chlorine in ClO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+3","+1","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chlorine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-21","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 5 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0155 mol","1500 mol","0.0311 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-22","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 333 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-23","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 16 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.364 mol","704.0 mol","2.750 mol","16.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-24","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.18 dm³ of a 0.09 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.648 g","3.600 g","7.200 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-25","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 8 dm³ at 6 atm. What volume will it occupy at 5 atm at constant temperature?","options":["9.6 dm³","6.66667 dm³","40 dm³","9 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-26","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 8 carbon atoms?","options":["C8H18","C8H16","C8H14","C9H18"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-27","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 10 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["8","10","12","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-28","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.005 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.30","3.30","11.70","1.30"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-29","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 72 g carbon, 12 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C3H6O1","C4H6O1","C3H7O1","C3H6O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-30","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chromium in Cr₂O₇²⁻?","options":["+6","+3","+4","+2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chromium.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 6 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0224 mol","2160 mol","0.0448 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 344 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-chemistry-2","title":"Chemistry UTME Standard CBT 2","subject":"Chemistry","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 50 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 50 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 50.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 49 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.86 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.86 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 43 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 49 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C49H100.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 54 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 54 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 54.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 53 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.94 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.94 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 47 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 53 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C53H108.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 7 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0087 mol","840 mol","0.0174 mol","0.05833 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 355 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 18 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.409 mol","792.0 mol","2.444 mol","18.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.22 dm³ of a 0.11 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.968 g","4.400 g","8.800 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 10 dm³ at 8 atm. What volume will it occupy at 7 atm at constant temperature?","options":["11.4286 dm³","8.75 dm³","70 dm³","11 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 10 carbon atoms?","options":["C10H22","C10H20","C10H18","C11H22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 12 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","12","13","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.007 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.15","3.15","11.85","1.15"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 96 g carbon, 16 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C8H16O1","C9H16O1","C8H17O1","C8H16O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of nitrogen in NO₂⁻?","options":["+3","+5","+1","−3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for nitrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 8 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0149 mol","1440 mol","0.0298 mol","0.04444 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 366 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 19 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.432 mol","836.0 mol","2.316 mol","19.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.24 dm³ of a 0.12 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.152 g","4.800 g","9.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 11 dm³ at 9 atm. What volume will it occupy at 8 atm at constant temperature?","options":["12.375 dm³","9.77778 dm³","88 dm³","12 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 11 carbon atoms?","options":["C11H24","C11H22","C11H20","C12H24"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 13 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","13","15","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.008 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.10","3.10","11.90","1.10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 108 g carbon, 18 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C4H9O1","C5H9O1","C4H10O1","C4H9O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in SO₃²⁻?","options":["+4","+6","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 9 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0224 mol","2160 mol","0.0448 mol","0.03750 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 377 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 20 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.455 mol","880.0 mol","2.200 mol","20.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.26 dm³ of a 0.13 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.352 g","5.200 g","10.400 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-035","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 12 dm³ at 10 atm. What volume will it occupy at 1 atm at constant temperature?","options":["120 dm³","1.2 dm³","12 dm³","21 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 12 carbon atoms?","options":["C12H26","C12H24","C12H22","C13H26"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-041","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 14 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","14","17","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-044","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.009 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.05","3.05","11.95","1.05"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-046","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 120 g carbon, 20 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C3H7O1","C4H7O1","C3H8O1","C3H7O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-048","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of carbon in CO₃²⁻?","options":["+4","+2","+6","−4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for carbon.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-051","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 10 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0311 mol","3000 mol","0.0622 mol","0.03333 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-056","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 388 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-chemistry-3","title":"Chemistry UTME Standard CBT 3","subject":"Chemistry","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 90 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 90 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 90.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 89 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-029","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 1.66 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 1.66 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 83 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-030","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 89 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C89H180.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 94 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 94 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 94.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 93 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 1.74 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 1.74 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 87 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-032","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 93 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C93H188.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-058","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 11 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0410 mol","3960 mol","0.0821 mol","0.03056 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-061","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 399 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-064","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 22 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.500 mol","968.0 mol","2.000 mol","22.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-071","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.3 dm³ of a 0.15 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.800 g","6.000 g","12.000 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-073","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 14 dm³ at 12 atm. What volume will it occupy at 3 atm at constant temperature?","options":["56 dm³","3.5 dm³","42 dm³","23 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-074","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 14 carbon atoms?","options":["C14H30","C14H28","C14H26","C15H30"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-076","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 16 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["14","16","18","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-081","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.011 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.96","2.96","12.04","0.96"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-091","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 144 g carbon, 24 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C6H12O1","C7H12O1","C6H13O1","C6H12O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-094","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chlorine in ClO₄⁻?","options":["+7","+5","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chlorine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-101","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 12 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0149 mol","1440 mol","0.0298 mol","0.10000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-104","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 410 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-111","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 23 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.523 mol","1012.0 mol","1.913 mol","23.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-114","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.32 dm³ of a 0.16 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.048 g","6.400 g","12.800 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-121","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 15 dm³ at 13 atm. What volume will it occupy at 4 atm at constant temperature?","options":["48.75 dm³","4.61538 dm³","60 dm³","24 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 15 carbon atoms?","options":["C15H32","C15H30","C15H28","C16H32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1000","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 17 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["14","17","20","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.012 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.92","2.92","12.08","0.92"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 156 g carbon, 26 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C4H9O1","C5H9O1","C4H10O1","C4H9O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of bromine in BrO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+7","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for bromine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 13 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0242 mol","2340 mol","0.0485 mol","0.07222 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 421 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 24 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.545 mol","1056.0 mol","1.833 mol","24.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.34 dm³ of a 0.17 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.312 g","6.800 g","13.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 16 dm³ at 14 atm. What volume will it occupy at 5 atm at constant temperature?","options":["44.8 dm³","5.71429 dm³","80 dm³","25 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 16 carbon atoms?","options":["C16H34","C16H32","C16H30","C17H34"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 18 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["17","18","19","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.013 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.89","2.89","12.11","0.89"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 168 g carbon, 28 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C14H28O1","C15H28O1","C14H29O1","C14H28O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of iodine in IO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+7","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for iodine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 14 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0348 mol","3360 mol","0.0696 mol","0.05833 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 432 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-chemistry-4","title":"Chemistry UTME Standard CBT 4","subject":"Chemistry","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 130 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 130 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 130.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 129 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-033","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 2.46 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 2.46 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 123 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 129 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C129H260.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-015","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 134 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 134 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 134.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 133 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-035","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 2.54 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 2.54 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 127 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 133 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C133H268.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 15 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0466 mol","4500 mol","0.0933 mol","0.05000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 443 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1020","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 26 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.591 mol","1144.0 mol","1.692 mol","26.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.38 dm³ of a 0.19 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.888 g","7.600 g","15.200 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 18 dm³ at 16 atm. What volume will it occupy at 7 atm at constant temperature?","options":["41.1429 dm³","7.875 dm³","126 dm³","27 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 18 carbon atoms?","options":["C18H38","C18H36","C18H34","C19H38"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 20 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["17","20","23","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.015 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.82","2.82","12.18","0.82"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 192 g carbon, 32 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C5H11O1","C6H11O1","C5H12O1","C5H11O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1030","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of carbon in CO₂?","options":["+4","+2","+6","−4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for carbon.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 16 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0597 mol","5760 mol","0.1194 mol","0.04444 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 454 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 27 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.614 mol","1188.0 mol","1.630 mol","27.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1037","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.4 dm³ of a 0.2 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["3.200 g","8.000 g","16.000 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1038","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 19 dm³ at 17 atm. What volume will it occupy at 8 atm at constant temperature?","options":["40.375 dm³","8.94118 dm³","152 dm³","28 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1040","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 19 carbon atoms?","options":["C19H40","C19H38","C19H36","C20H40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1041","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 21 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["20","21","22","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1044","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.016 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.80","2.80","12.20","0.80"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1046","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 204 g carbon, 34 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C17H34O1","C18H34O1","C17H35O1","C17H34O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1047","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in H₂SO₄?","options":["+6","+4","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1048","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 17 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0211 mol","2040 mol","0.0423 mol","0.14167 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1050","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 465 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1051","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 28 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.636 mol","1232.0 mol","1.571 mol","28.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1054","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.42 dm³ of a 0.21 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["3.528 g","8.400 g","16.800 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1056","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 20 dm³ at 18 atm. What volume will it occupy at 1 atm at constant temperature?","options":["360 dm³","1.11111 dm³","20 dm³","37 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1057","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 20 carbon atoms?","options":["C20H42","C20H40","C20H38","C21H42"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1058","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 22 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["20","22","24","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1060","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.017 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.77","2.77","12.23","0.77"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1061","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 216 g carbon, 36 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C9H18O1","C10H18O1","C9H19O1","C9H18O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1064","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chlorine in ClO⁻?","options":["+1","+3","+5","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chlorine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1067","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 18 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0336 mol","3240 mol","0.0672 mol","0.10000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1068","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 476 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-chemistry-5","title":"Chemistry UTME Standard CBT 5","subject":"Chemistry","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 170 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 170 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 170.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 169 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-037","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 3.26 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 3.26 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 163 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-038","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 169 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C169H340.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-019","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 174 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 174 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 174.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 173 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-039","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 3.34 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 3.34 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-020","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 167 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-040","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 173 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C173H348.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1070","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 19 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0473 mol","4560 mol","0.0945 mol","0.07917 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1074","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 487 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1077","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 30 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.682 mol","1320.0 mol","1.467 mol","30.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1078","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.46 dm³ of a 0.23 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["4.232 g","9.200 g","18.400 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1084","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 22 dm³ at 20 atm. What volume will it occupy at 3 atm at constant temperature?","options":["146.667 dm³","3.3 dm³","66 dm³","39 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1087","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 22 carbon atoms?","options":["C22H46","C22H44","C22H42","C23H46"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1088","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 24 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["23","24","25","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1094","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.019 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.72","2.72","12.28","0.72"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1097","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 240 g carbon, 40 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C20H40O1","C21H40O1","C20H41O1","C20H40O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1098","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of phosphorus in PCl₅?","options":["+5","+3","−3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for phosphorus.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1104","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 20 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0622 mol","6000 mol","0.1244 mol","0.06667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1107","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 498 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1108","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 31 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.705 mol","1364.0 mol","1.419 mol","31.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1114","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.48 dm³ of a 0.24 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["4.608 g","9.600 g","19.200 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1117","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 23 dm³ at 21 atm. What volume will it occupy at 4 atm at constant temperature?","options":["120.75 dm³","4.38095 dm³","92 dm³","40 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1118","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 23 carbon atoms?","options":["C23H48","C23H46","C23H44","C24H48"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 25 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["23","25","27","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1127","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.02 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.70","2.70","12.30","0.70"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1128","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 252 g carbon, 42 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C10H21O1","C11H21O1","C10H22O1","C10H21O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1134","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of carbon in CH₄?","options":["−4","+4","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for carbon.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1137","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 21 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0783 mol","7560 mol","0.1567 mol","0.05833 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1138","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 509 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1144","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 32 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.727 mol","1408.0 mol","1.375 mol","32.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1147","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.5 dm³ of a 0.25 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["5.000 g","10.000 g","20.000 g","Insufficient information to determine the answer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1148","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 24 dm³ at 22 atm. What volume will it occupy at 5 atm at constant temperature?","options":["105.6 dm³","5.45455 dm³","120 dm³","41 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1154","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 24 carbon atoms?","options":["C24H50","C24H48","C24H46","C25H50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1157","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 26 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["23","26","29","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1158","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.021 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.68","2.68","12.32","0.68"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1164","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 264 g carbon, 44 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C7H15O1","C8H15O1","C7H16O1","C7H15O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1167","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of nitrogen in N₂O?","options":["+1","+3","−1","−3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for nitrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1168","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 22 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0274 mol","2640 mol","0.0547 mol","0.18333 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1174","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 520 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-biology-1","title":"Biology UTME Standard CBT 1","subject":"Biology","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the plasma membrane of a liver cell maintained at approximately 25 °C are correct?\n\nI. The membrane is selectively permeable.\nII. Phospholipids form the basic bilayer.\nIII. Some membrane proteins facilitate movement of substances.\nIV. Every substance crosses the membrane by simple diffusion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; some substances require facilitated diffusion or active transport.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about inheritance in a diploid organism with 3 chromosome pairs are correct?\n\nI. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\nII. Meiosis can generate new allele combinations.\nIII. DNA carries hereditary information.\nIV. Every mutation produces a harmful phenotype.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; mutations may be harmful, neutral or beneficial.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a population of 1400 organisms are correct?\n\nI. Births can increase population size.\nII. Deaths can reduce population size.\nIII. Immigration can increase population size.\nIV. Emigration always increases population size.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; emigration removes individuals from the population.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about human regulation are correct when blood glucose is approximately 79 mg/dL?\n\nI. Insulin helps lower blood glucose concentration.\nII. ADH promotes water reabsorption in the kidney.\nIII. Negative feedback helps maintain internal stability.\nIV. Hormones act only on cells that have no receptors for them.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; hormones exert effects through appropriate receptors.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the plasma membrane of a guard cell maintained at approximately 29 °C are correct?\n\nI. The membrane is selectively permeable.\nII. Phospholipids form the basic bilayer.\nIII. Some membrane proteins facilitate movement of substances.\nIV. Every substance crosses the membrane by simple diffusion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; some substances require facilitated diffusion or active transport.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about inheritance in a diploid organism with 7 chromosome pairs are correct?\n\nI. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\nII. Meiosis can generate new allele combinations.\nIII. DNA carries hereditary information.\nIV. Every mutation produces a harmful phenotype.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; mutations may be harmful, neutral or beneficial.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a population of 1800 organisms are correct?\n\nI. Births can increase population size.\nII. Deaths can reduce population size.\nIII. Immigration can increase population size.\nIV. Emigration always increases population size.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; emigration removes individuals from the population.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about human regulation are correct when blood glucose is approximately 91 mg/dL?\n\nI. Insulin helps lower blood glucose concentration.\nII. ADH promotes water reabsorption in the kidney.\nIII. Negative feedback helps maintain internal stability.\nIV. Hormones act only on cells that have no receptors for them.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; hormones exert effects through appropriate receptors.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-01","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a large randomly mating population, the frequency of an autosomal recessive allele is 0.06. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg conditions, what is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","options":["0.11","0.00","0.88","1.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With q as the recessive-allele frequency, p=1−q and heterozygotes occur at 2pq.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-02","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a producer stores 10,500 kJ of chemical energy and approximately 10% is transferred to the next trophic level, how much energy is available there?","options":["1,050 kJ","105 kJ","5,250 kJ","9,500 kJ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using the ecological efficiency approximation, 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-03","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a cross between two heterozygous plants with alleles P and p, where P is dominant for purple and p is recessive for white, what proportion of offspring is expected to show white?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A heterozygous × heterozygous cross gives a 1:2:1 genotype ratio and therefore 25% recessive phenotype.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-04","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A plant transpires at an average rate of 4 cm³ of water per hour for 7 hours. How much water is lost in that period?","options":["28 cm³","11 cm³","0.57 cm³","32 cm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total water loss = rate × time.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-05","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a healthy adult, alveolar oxygen partial pressure is 89 mmHg while venous blood oxygen partial pressure is 37 mmHg. Which gas will diffuse from the alveolus into the blood?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Oxygen moves down its partial-pressure gradient from alveolar air into venous blood.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-06","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which process most directly contributes to genetic variation by metaphase I of meiosis?","options":["independent assortment","binary fission","mitosis","cytokinesis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent assortment contributes to genetic variation in the stated context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-07","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A population contains 2200 organisms. In one year there are 134 births, 78 deaths, 36 immigrants and 24 emigrants. What is the net increase?","options":["68","56","12","78"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Net population change = births − deaths + immigration − emigration.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-08","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which hormone is most directly associated with the following physiological effect: raise blood glucose during fasting?","options":["glucagon","insulin","thyroxine","ADH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"glucagon has the stated physiological role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-09","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which structure is best adapted to provide a large surface area for gas exchange?","options":["alveoli — provide a large surface area for gas exchange","phloem sieve tubes — translocate organic solutes","lysosomes — digest cellular materials","cartilage — reduce friction at joints"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alveoli is specialised for the stated function.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-10","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option correctly matches phloem with its principal role?","options":["phloem — organic solutes","neurones — nerve impulses","guard cells — stomatal regulation","pancreas — digestive enzymes and hormones"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The selected structure is correctly matched with its principal role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-11","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a large randomly mating population, the frequency of an autosomal recessive allele is 0.07. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg conditions, what is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","options":["0.13","0.00","0.86","1.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With q as the recessive-allele frequency, p=1−q and heterozygotes occur at 2pq.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-12","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a producer stores 11,000 kJ of chemical energy and approximately 10% is transferred to the next trophic level, how much energy is available there?","options":["1,100 kJ","110 kJ","5,500 kJ","10,000 kJ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using the ecological efficiency approximation, 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-13","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a cross between two heterozygous plants with alleles R and r, where R is dominant for round and r is recessive for wrinkled, what proportion of offspring is expected to show wrinkled?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A heterozygous × heterozygous cross gives a 1:2:1 genotype ratio and therefore 25% recessive phenotype.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-14","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A plant transpires at an average rate of 5 cm³ of water per hour for 8 hours. How much water is lost in that period?","options":["40 cm³","13 cm³","0.62 cm³","45 cm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total water loss = rate × time.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-15","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a healthy adult, alveolar oxygen partial pressure is 91 mmHg while venous blood oxygen partial pressure is 38 mmHg. Which gas will diffuse from the alveolus into the blood?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Oxygen moves down its partial-pressure gradient from alveolar air into venous blood.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-16","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which process most directly contributes to genetic variation by fertilisation?","options":["random fertilisation","binary fission","mitosis","cytokinesis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Random fertilisation contributes to genetic variation in the stated context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-17","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A population contains 2300 organisms. In one year there are 141 births, 82 deaths, 39 immigrants and 26 emigrants. What is the net increase?","options":["72","59","13","82"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Net population change = births − deaths + immigration − emigration.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-18","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which hormone is most directly associated with the following physiological effect: increase water reabsorption by the kidneys?","options":["ADH","insulin","thyroxine","cortisol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ADH has the stated physiological role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-19","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which structure is best adapted to increase the surface area for mineral and water absorption?","options":["root hairs — increase the surface area for mineral and water absorption","red blood cells — transport oxygen efficiently","guard cells — control stomatal opening","tendons — attach muscles to bones"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Root hairs is specialised for the stated function.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-20","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option correctly matches red blood cells with its principal role?","options":["red blood cells — oxygen","ciliated epithelial cells — movement of mucus","sperm cells — fertilisation of the ovum","trachea — air passage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The selected structure is correctly matched with its principal role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-21","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a large randomly mating population, the frequency of an autosomal recessive allele is 0.08. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg conditions, what is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","options":["0.15","0.01","0.85","1.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With q as the recessive-allele frequency, p=1−q and heterozygotes occur at 2pq.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-22","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a producer stores 11,500 kJ of chemical energy and approximately 10% is transferred to the next trophic level, how much energy is available there?","options":["1,150 kJ","115 kJ","5,750 kJ","10,500 kJ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using the ecological efficiency approximation, 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-23","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a cross between two heterozygous plants with alleles Y and y, where Y is dominant for yellow and y is recessive for green, what proportion of offspring is expected to show green?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A heterozygous × heterozygous cross gives a 1:2:1 genotype ratio and therefore 25% recessive phenotype.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-24","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A plant transpires at an average rate of 6 cm³ of water per hour for 9 hours. How much water is lost in that period?","options":["54 cm³","15 cm³","0.67 cm³","60 cm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total water loss = rate × time.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-25","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a healthy adult, alveolar oxygen partial pressure is 93 mmHg while venous blood oxygen partial pressure is 39 mmHg. Which gas will diffuse from the alveolus into the blood?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Oxygen moves down its partial-pressure gradient from alveolar air into venous blood.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-26","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which process most directly contributes to genetic variation by DNA sequence?","options":["mutation","binary fission","mitosis","cytokinesis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mutation contributes to genetic variation in the stated context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-27","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A population contains 2400 organisms. In one year there are 148 births, 86 deaths, 42 immigrants and 28 emigrants. What is the net increase?","options":["76","62","14","86"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Net population change = births − deaths + immigration − emigration.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-28","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which hormone is most directly associated with the following physiological effect: increase basal metabolic activity?","options":["thyroxine","insulin","adrenaline","ADH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"thyroxine has the stated physiological role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-29","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which structure is best adapted to filter blood and form urine?","options":["nephrons — filter blood and form urine","mitochondria — carry out aerobic respiration","myelin sheath — speed up impulse conduction","ligaments — connect bone to bone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nephrons is specialised for the stated function.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-biology-30","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which option correctly matches white blood cells with its principal role?","options":["white blood cells — immune defence","muscle fibres — contraction","ovaries — ova and female sex hormones","alveoli — gas exchange"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The selected structure is correctly matched with its principal role.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a large randomly mating population, the frequency of an autosomal recessive allele is 0.09. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg conditions, what is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","options":["0.16","0.01","0.83","1.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With q as the recessive-allele frequency, p=1−q and heterozygotes occur at 2pq.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a producer stores 12,000 kJ of chemical energy and approximately 10% is transferred to the next trophic level, how much energy is available there?","options":["1,200 kJ","120 kJ","6,000 kJ","11,000 kJ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using the ecological efficiency approximation, 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false}],"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"test-biology-2","title":"Biology UTME Standard CBT 2","subject":"Biology","duration":40,"published":true,"shuffleQuestions":true,"shuffleOptions":true,"questions":[{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the plasma membrane of a liver cell maintained at approximately 65 °C are correct?\n\nI. The membrane is selectively permeable.\nII. Phospholipids form the basic bilayer.\nIII. Some membrane proteins facilitate movement of substances.\nIV. Every substance crosses the membrane by simple diffusion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; some substances require facilitated diffusion or active transport.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about inheritance in a diploid organism with 43 chromosome pairs are correct?\n\nI. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\nII. Meiosis can generate new allele combinations.\nIII. DNA carries hereditary information.\nIV. Every mutation produces a harmful phenotype.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; mutations may be harmful, neutral or beneficial.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a population of 5400 organisms are correct?\n\nI. Births can increase population size.\nII. Deaths can reduce population size.\nIII. Immigration can increase population size.\nIV. Emigration always increases population size.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; emigration removes individuals from the population.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about human regulation are correct when blood glucose is approximately 199 mg/dL?\n\nI. Insulin helps lower blood glucose concentration.\nII. ADH promotes water reabsorption in the kidney.\nIII. Negative feedback helps maintain internal stability.\nIV. Hormones act only on cells that have no receptors for them.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; hormones exert effects through appropriate receptors.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the plasma membrane of a guard cell maintained at approximately 69 °C are correct?\n\nI. The membrane is selectively permeable.\nII. Phospholipids form the basic bilayer.\nIII. Some membrane proteins facilitate movement of substances.\nIV. Every substance crosses the membrane by simple diffusion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; some substances require facilitated diffusion or active transport.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about inheritance in a diploid organism with 47 chromosome pairs are correct?\n\nI. Alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\nII. Meiosis can generate new allele combinations.\nIII. DNA carries hereditary information.\nIV. Every mutation produces a harmful phenotype.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; mutations may be harmful, neutral or beneficial.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about a population of 5800 organisms are correct?\n\nI. Births can increase population size.\nII. Deaths can reduce population size.\nIII. Immigration can increase population size.\nIV. Emigration always increases population size.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; emigration removes individuals from the population.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-biology-roman-clean-028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about human regulation are correct when blood glucose is approximately 211 mg/dL?\n\nI. Insulin helps lower blood glucose concentration.\nII. ADH promotes water reabsorption in the kidney.\nIII. Negative feedback helps maintain internal stability.\nIV. Hormones act only on cells that have no receptors for them.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; hormones exert effects through appropriate receptors.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":true},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a large randomly mating population, the frequency of an autosomal recessive allele is 0.10. Assuming Hardy–Weinberg conditions, what is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","options":["0.18","0.01","0.81","1.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With q as the recessive-allele frequency, p=1−q and heterozygotes occur at 2pq.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"If a producer stores 12,500 kJ of chemical energy and approximately 10% is transferred to the next trophic level, how much energy is available there?","options":["1,250 kJ","125 kJ","6,250 kJ","11,500 kJ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using the ecological efficiency approximation, 10% of the energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a cross between two heterozygous plants with alleles H and h, where H is dominant for hard and h is recessive for soft, what proportion of offspring is expected to show soft?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A heterozygous × heterozygous cross gives a 1:2:1 genotype ratio and therefore 25% recessive phenotype.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A plant transpires at an average rate of 3 cm³ of water per hour for 11 hours. How much water is lost in that period?","options":["33 cm³","14 cm³","0.27 cm³","36 cm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total water loss = rate × time.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"In a healthy adult, alveolar oxygen partial pressure is 97 mmHg while venous blood oxygen partial pressure is 35 mmHg. Which gas will diffuse from the alveolus into the blood?","options":["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Oxygen moves down its partial-pressure gradient from alveolar air into venous blood.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which process most directly contributes to genetic variation by homologous chromosomes?","options":["recombination","binary fission","mitosis","cytokinesis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recombination contributes to genetic variation in the stated context.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Biology","roman":false},{"id":"qb-bio-gen-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A population contains 2600 organisms. In one year there are 162 births, 94 deaths, 48 immigrants and 