 change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-120","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 6 Ω carries a current of 21 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["2646 W","126 W","27 W","0.285714 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-121","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 24 Hz and wavelength 0.35 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["8.4 m/s","68.5714 m/s","24.35 m/s","0.0145833 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-122","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 5 kg object moves at 23 m/s in a circle of radius 21 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["125.952 N","5.47619 N","2415 N","55545 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-123","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.16 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.25 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.183 m","0.142 m","1.090 m","0.200 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 21 kg of water by 8 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["705,600 J","88,200 J","33,600 J","70,560.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-125","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 700 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["2.841e-19 J","1.547e-48 J","1.421e-19 J","1.392e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-126","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A car travels with initial speed 28 m/s and uniform acceleration 2 m/s² for 5 s. What distance does it cover?","options":["165 m","140 m","190 m","25 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use s = ut + ½at².","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-127","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 22 kg object is acted upon by a resultant force of 110 N. What acceleration does it acquire?","options":["5 m/s²","110 m/s²","22 m/s²","6 m/s²"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newton’s second law gives a=F/m.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-128","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A load of mass 23 kg is lifted vertically through 6 m. Taking g=10 m/s², what increase in gravitational potential energy occurs?","options":["1380 J","138 J","230 J","690 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔPE=mgh.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-129","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 22 kg trolley increases its speed from 24 m/s to 29 m/s. What impulse acts on it?","options":["110 N s","638 N s","528 N s","1166 N s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum: m(v−u).","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-phys-gen-130","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A resistor of 7 Ω carries a current of 22 A. What electrical power is dissipated?","options":["3388 W","154 W","29 W","0.318182 W"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power P=I²R or VI.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1000","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A wave has frequency 25 Hz and wavelength 0.4 m. Calculate its speed.","options":["10 m/s","62.5 m/s","25.4 m/s","0.016 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wave speed v=fλ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A 1 kg object moves at 24 m/s in a circle of radius 22 m. What is the magnitude of its centripetal force?","options":["26.1818 N","1.09091 N","528 N","12672 N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force is mv²/r.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A converging lens has focal length 0.18 m and forms a real image when an object is 1.3 m from it. What is the image distance?","options":["0.209 m","0.158 m","1.120 m","0.234 m"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use the lens formula 1/f=1/u+1/v with consistent sign conventions for magnitudes in this real-object case.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 22 kg of water by 9 °C? Take c=4200 J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.","options":["831,600 J","92,400 J","37,800 J","83,160.0 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heat supplied Q=mcΔT.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-physics-gen-1004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Light of wavelength 720 nm falls on a surface. Using h=6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s and c=3.0×10⁸ m/s, what is the energy of one photon?","options":["2.762e-19 J","1.591e-48 J","1.381e-19 J","1.432e-31 J"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Photon energy is E=hc/λ.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Physics","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 10 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 10 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 10.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 9 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.06 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.06 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 3 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 9 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C9H20.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 14 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 14 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 14.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 13 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.14 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.14 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 7 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 13 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C13H28.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-01","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 3 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0056 mol","540 mol","0.0112 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-02","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 311 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-03","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 14 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.318 mol","616.0 mol","3.143 mol","14.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-04","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.14 dm³ of a 0.07 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.392 g","2.800 g","5.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-05","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 6 dm³ at 4 atm. What volume will it occupy at 3 atm at constant temperature?","options":["8 dm³","4.5 dm³","18 dm³","7 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-06","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 6 carbon atoms?","options":["C6H14","C6H12","C6H10","C7H14"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-07","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 8 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["5","8","11","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-08","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.003 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.52","3.52","11.48","1.52"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-09","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 48 g carbon, 8 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C1H3O1","C2H3O1","C1H4O1","C1H3O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-10","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in SO₄²⁻?","options":["+6","+4","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-11","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 4 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0099 mol","960 mol","0.0199 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-12","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 322 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-13","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 15 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.341 mol","660.0 mol","2.933 mol","15.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-14","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.16 dm³ of a 0.08 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.512 g","3.200 g","6.400 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-15","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 7 dm³ at 5 atm. What volume will it occupy at 4 atm at constant temperature?","options":["8.75 dm³","5.6 dm³","28 dm³","8 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-16","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 7 carbon atoms?","options":["C7H16","C7H14","C7H12","C8H16"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-17","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 9 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["8","9","10","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-18","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.004 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.40","3.40","11.60","1.40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-19","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 60 g carbon, 10 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C5H10O1","C6H10O1","C5H11O1","C5H10O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-20","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chlorine in ClO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+3","+1","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chlorine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-21","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 5 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0155 mol","1500 mol","0.0311 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-22","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 333 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-23","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 16 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.364 mol","704.0 mol","2.750 mol","16.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-24","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.18 dm³ of a 0.09 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.648 g","3.600 g","7.200 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-25","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 8 dm³ at 6 atm. What volume will it occupy at 5 atm at constant temperature?","options":["9.6 dm³","6.66667 dm³","40 dm³","9 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-26","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 8 carbon atoms?","options":["C8H18","C8H16","C8H14","C9H18"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-27","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 10 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["8","10","12","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-28","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.005 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.30","3.30","11.70","1.30"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-29","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 72 g carbon, 12 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C3H6O1","C4H6O1","C3H7O1","C3H6O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-30","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chromium in Cr₂O₇²⁻?","options":["+6","+3","+4","+2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chromium.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 6 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0224 mol","2160 mol","0.0448 mol","0.01667 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 344 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 50 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 50 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 50.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 49 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.86 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.86 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 43 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 49 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C49H100.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 54 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 54 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 54.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 53 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 0.94 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 0.94 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 47 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 53 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C53H108.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 7 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0087 mol","840 mol","0.0174 mol","0.05833 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 355 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 18 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.409 mol","792.0 mol","2.444 mol","18.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.22 dm³ of a 0.11 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["0.968 g","4.400 g","8.800 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 10 dm³ at 8 atm. What volume will it occupy at 7 atm at constant temperature?","options":["11.4286 dm³","8.75 dm³","70 dm³","11 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 10 carbon atoms?","options":["C10H22","C10H20","C10H18","C11H22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 12 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","12","13","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.007 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.15","3.15","11.85","1.15"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 96 g carbon, 16 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C8H16O1","C9H16O1","C8H17O1","C8H16O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of nitrogen in NO₂⁻?","options":["+3","+5","+1","−3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for nitrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 8 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0149 mol","1440 mol","0.0298 mol","0.04444 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 366 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 19 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.432 mol","836.0 mol","2.316 mol","19.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.24 dm³ of a 0.12 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.152 g","4.800 g","9.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 11 dm³ at 9 atm. What volume will it occupy at 8 atm at constant temperature?","options":["12.375 dm³","9.77778 dm³","88 dm³","12 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 11 carbon atoms?","options":["C11H24","C11H22","C11H20","C12H24"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-022","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 13 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","13","15","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.008 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.10","3.10","11.90","1.10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 108 g carbon, 18 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C4H9O1","C5H9O1","C4H10O1","C4H9O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-025","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in SO₃²⁻?","options":["+4","+6","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 9 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0224 mol","2160 mol","0.0448 mol","0.03750 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 377 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 20 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.455 mol","880.0 mol","2.200 mol","20.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.26 dm³ of a 0.13 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.352 g","5.200 g","10.400 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-035","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 12 dm³ at 10 atm. What volume will it occupy at 1 atm at constant temperature?","options":["120 dm³","1.2 dm³","12 dm³","21 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 12 carbon atoms?","options":["C12H26","C12H24","C12H22","C13H26"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-041","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 14 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["11","14","17","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-044","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.009 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["2.05","3.05","11.95","1.05"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-046","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 120 g carbon, 20 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C3H7O1","C4H7O1","C3H8O1","C3H7O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-048","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of carbon in CO₃²⁻?","options":["+4","+2","+6","−4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for carbon.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-051","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 10 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0311 mol","3000 mol","0.0622 mol","0.03333 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-056","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 388 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 90 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 90 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 90.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 89 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-029","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 1.66 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 1.66 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 83 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-030","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 89 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C89H180.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 94 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 94 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 94.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 93 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 1.74 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 1.74 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 87 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-032","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 93 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C93H188.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-058","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 11 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0410 mol","3960 mol","0.0821 mol","0.03056 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-061","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 399 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-064","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 22 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.500 mol","968.0 mol","2.000 mol","22.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-071","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.3 dm³ of a 0.15 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["1.800 g","6.000 g","12.000 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-073","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 14 dm³ at 12 atm. What volume will it occupy at 3 atm at constant temperature?","options":["56 dm³","3.5 dm³","42 dm³","23 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-074","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 14 carbon atoms?","options":["C14H30","C14H28","C14H26","C15H30"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-076","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 16 and a charge of 2+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["14","16","18","2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-081","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.011 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.96","2.96","12.04","0.96"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-091","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 144 g carbon, 24 g hydrogen and 32 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C6H12O1","C7H12O1","C6H13O1","C6H12O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-094","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of chlorine in ClO₄⁻?","options":["+7","+5","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for chlorine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-101","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 12 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0149 mol","1440 mol","0.0298 mol","0.10000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-104","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 410 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-111","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 23 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.523 mol","1012.0 mol","1.913 mol","23.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-114","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.32 dm³ of a 0.16 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.048 g","6.400 g","12.800 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-121","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 15 dm³ at 13 atm. What volume will it occupy at 4 atm at constant temperature?","options":["48.75 dm³","4.61538 dm³","60 dm³","24 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chem-gen-124","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 15 carbon atoms?","options":["C15H32","C15H30","C15H28","C16H32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1000","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 17 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["14","17","20","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1001","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.012 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.92","2.92","12.08","0.92"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1002","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 156 g carbon, 26 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C4H9O1","C5H9O1","C4H10O1","C4H9O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1003","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of bromine in BrO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+7","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for bromine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1004","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 13 A flows for 180 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0242 mol","2340 mol","0.0485 mol","0.07222 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1005","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 421 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1006","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 24 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.545 mol","1056.0 mol","1.833 mol","24.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1007","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.34 dm³ of a 0.17 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.312 g","6.800 g","13.600 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1008","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 16 dm³ at 14 atm. What volume will it occupy at 5 atm at constant temperature?","options":["44.8 dm³","5.71429 dm³","80 dm³","25 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1009","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 16 carbon atoms?","options":["C16H34","C16H32","C16H30","C17H34"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1010","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 18 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["17","18","19","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1011","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.013 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.89","2.89","12.11","0.89"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1012","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 168 g carbon, 28 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C14H28O1","C15H28O1","C14H29O1","C14H28O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of iodine in IO₃⁻?","options":["+5","+7","+3","−1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for iodine.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 14 A flows for 240 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0348 mol","3360 mol","0.0696 mol","0.05833 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 432 K, for the equilibrium CO + H₂O ⇌ CO₂ + H₂, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["removing a product","adding more product","adding a catalyst to shift equilibrium","removing a reactant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Removing a product shifts equilibrium towards products to oppose the change.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-013","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 130 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 130 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 130.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 129 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-033","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 2.46 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 2.46 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-014","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of potassium iodide using a current of 123 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 129 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C129H260.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-015","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an element with atomic number 134 are correct?\n\nI. Its neutral atom contains 134 electrons.\nII. Its proton number is 134.\nIII. A +1 ion of the element contains 133 electrons.\nIV. Its atomic number changes when it forms an ion.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ion formation changes electron number, not atomic number.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-035","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about 2.54 mol dm⁻³ of a strong monoprotic acid are correct?\n\nI. It ionises extensively in water.\nII. Its hydrogen-ion concentration is approximately 2.54 mol dm⁻³.\nIII. Its pH is determined from −log[H⁺].\nIV. Complete ionisation means the acid contains no hydrogen atoms.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ionisation in water does not mean the acid molecule contains no hydrogen.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-016","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of lead(II) bromide using a current of 127 A are correct?\n\nI. Reduction occurs at the cathode.\nII. Oxidation occurs at the anode.\nIII. Cations migrate towards the cathode.\nIV. Electrons are the principal charge carriers throughout the electrolyte solution.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; ions carry charge through the electrolyte.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-roman-clean-036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which statements about an alkane containing 133 carbon atoms are correct?\n\nI. Its general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.\nII. Its molecular formula is C133H268.\nIII. It is a saturated hydrocarbon.\nIV. It must contain a carbon–carbon triple bond.","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"I, II and III are correct; alkanes are saturated and do not contain triple bonds.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":true},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1017","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 15 A flows for 300 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0466 mol","4500 mol","0.0933 mol","0.05000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1018","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 443 K, for the equilibrium N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["lowering the temperature","raising the temperature","adding a catalyst","removing nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For an exothermic forward reaction, lowering temperature favours the product side.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1020","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 26 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.591 mol","1144.0 mol","1.692 mol","26.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1021","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.38 dm³ of a 0.19 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["2.888 g","7.600 g","15.200 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1023","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 18 dm³ at 16 atm. What volume will it occupy at 7 atm at constant temperature?","options":["41.1429 dm³","7.875 dm³","126 dm³","27 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1024","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 18 carbon atoms?","options":["C18H38","C18H36","C18H34","C19H38"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1026","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 20 and a charge of 3+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["17","20","23","3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1027","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.015 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.82","2.82","12.18","0.82"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1028","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 192 g carbon, 32 g hydrogen and 48 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C5H11O1","C6H11O1","C5H12O1","C5H11O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1030","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of carbon in CO₂?","options":["+4","+2","+6","−4"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for carbon.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1031","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 16 A flows for 360 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0597 mol","5760 mol","0.1194 mol","0.04444 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1034","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 454 K, for the equilibrium 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["increasing the pressure","decreasing the pressure","adding an inert gas at constant volume","raising the temperature for an exothermic forward reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The product side has fewer gas molecules, so increasing pressure favours products.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1036","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 27 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.614 mol","1188.0 mol","1.630 mol","27.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1037","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to prepare 0.4 dm³ of a 0.2 mol dm⁻³ solution?","options":["3.200 g","8.000 g","16.000 g","20.000 g"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use n=CV and mass=nMr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1038","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A gas occupies 19 dm³ at 17 atm. What volume will it occupy at 8 atm at constant temperature?","options":["40.375 dm³","8.94118 dm³","152 dm³","28 dm³"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boyle’s law: P₁V₁=P₂V₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1040","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"Which molecular formula represents the alkane containing 19 carbon atoms?","options":["C19H40","C19H38","C19H36","C20H40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alkanes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1041","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"An ion has atomic number 21 and a charge of 1+. How many electrons does it contain?","options":["20","21","22","1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive ion has lost electrons, so electrons=atomic number−charge.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1044","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A strong monoprotic acid has concentration 0.016 mol dm⁻³. What is its approximate pH?","options":["1.80","2.80","12.20","0.80"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a strong monoprotic acid, [H⁺]≈c and pH=−log[H⁺].","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1046","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A compound contains 204 g carbon, 34 g hydrogen and 16 g oxygen. What is its empirical formula?","options":["C17H34O1","C18H34O1","C17H35O1","C17H34O2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert each mass to moles, divide by the smallest mole value and reduce to the simplest whole-number ratio.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1047","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in H₂SO₄?","options":["+6","+4","+2","−2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assign the usual oxidation numbers to the other atoms and use the overall charge of the species to solve for sulfur.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1048","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A current of 17 A flows for 120 s. Approximately how many moles of electrons pass through the circuit? Take F=96,500 C mol⁻¹.","options":["0.0211 mol","2040 mol","0.0423 mol","0.14167 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Charge Q=It; moles of electrons=Q/F.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1050","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"At 465 K, for the equilibrium H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, which change would favour the products under the stated equilibrium conditions?","options":["changing concentrations according to Le Chatelier’s principle","using a catalyst to change Kc","removing all reactants","stopping the reaction at equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing concentrations can shift equilibrium according to Le Chatelier’s principle; a catalyst does not change equilibrium position.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1051","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"A sample of carbon dioxide has a mass of 28 g. If Mr(CO₂)=44, how many moles of CO₂ are present?","options":["0.636 mol","1232.0 mol","1.571 mol","28.000 mol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Moles = mass/Mr.","image":null,"source":"Gifted Brainz UTME Standard Question Bank 2026","needsReview":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z","subject":"Chemistry","roman":false},{"id":"qb-chemistry-gen-1054","type":"mcq","passageId":"","passage":"","q":"What mass of NaOH (Mr=40) is required to pre